window.harnessChunks["rows-a1e80a89684a212d.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:51:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orka</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/orka-agents/orka/tree/7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cffe130efa74f505d66dce561ade6c18901ba6e\">7cffe13 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out user-directed AI tasks, generate code/reviews and use first-party registered tools to produce work artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous prompts, code/test state, model/tool results, errors and task-specific context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated AI worker or coordinator chooses content-bearing tool calls and task actions based on returned observations. - Decision owner: Orka-instantiated model-driven AI Task/coordinator; external coding CLIs keep their own lower-level agent loops and are not the sole witness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes Task CRD, pod lifecycle, credentials, workspace, provider proxy, RBAC, task timeout and durable result store. - Closure path: user/Task prompt → worker/model decision → tool or delegated action → pod/run result → subsequent model decision or returned artifact. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s in-cluster type: ai, chat request handling and registered create/execute tools are supported paths, not only demos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven selection resolves task content; fixed pod orchestration and auth do not substitute for S1 discretion. - Evidence: README; chat.go; chattoolexecutor.go; general worker. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among sibling specialist Tasks for a configured coordinator mission, including redundant race candidates and result-dependent message handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated coder, reviewer, tester or peer Tasks under one coordinator/transaction, each capable of independently performing work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: redundant simultaneously running alternatives consuming capacity, stale handoffs and sibling actions on incompatible intermediate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator may select the first useful race result and cancel other running siblings; configured send/check message and explicit dependency handoff give sibling work a shared decision route instead of independent conflicting continuation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waitfortasks returns terminal child status/retry metadata, canceltask stops no-longer-useful work and checkmessages changes a receiver&#39;s next step. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a selected collision/duplication-interference pattern with returned control into separately executing S1s, not generic parallelism or sequential tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: race/redundancy and incoherent sibling outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which running child to retain or stop and when an intermediate handoff is safe. - Decision owner: model-driven coordinator only when a coordination-enabled Agent and corresponding task patterns are configured; primitives supply the constructor but no global automatic S2 is assumed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child identity, scoped task authorization, cancellation API, durable messaging and structured terminal waits. - Closure path: concurrent child observations → coordinator judgment about winner/dependency → cancel/message/wait invocation → sibling next behavior/terminal status. - Boundary reachability: coordinated execution and control are shipped as tools, not only example YAML; a configured coordinator can call them via the first-party session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can make the interference choice in the composed race mode; generic messenger/Task storage alone do not supply a mandatory organization-wide coordinator, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: coordination section, registered delegate, wait, cancel and messages. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current delegated mission, its specialist task status and correction loop, rather than all independent Kubernetes namespaces. - Whole-system current view: the configured coordinator observes the complete active mission&#39;s child-task status, rework, validation and review outcomes, not unaffiliated namespace workloads. - Current-control decision scope: current mission task assignment, retry, rework, cancellation, blocked disposition and completion readiness under the original user&#39;s authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed specialist deliverables, validation/review failures, blocked Tasks and incomplete current mission steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven coordinator decides retry/rework/delegation/cancellation from returned child/evaluation feedback and decides when to finalize or report a blocked mission. - Decision owner: configured coordinator agent; platform timeouts and allowlists remain subordinate enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: waitfortasks terminal returns, validation and PR-CI tools, task/agent creation and update, task records, bounded retry budgets in product guidance. - Closure path: mission plan → delegated work → wait/validate/review/CI feedback → coordinator correction/delegation → revised task outputs and terminal mission disposition. - Boundary reachability: createagent coordination and optional initialPrompt initiate a coordinator and subordinate task using the supported API, and the shipped coding workflow guidance explicitly loops over failed validation/reviews before PR/CI closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator interprets heterogeneous current results and chooses corrections, not just fixed scheduling; no all-installation budget/policy S3 is claimed. - Evidence: chatsystemprompt.go; delegatetask.go; waitfortasks.go. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the working PR/task outcome against exact-head evidence, then make findings consequential for mission readiness and rework. - Claim being audited: a coder&#39;s PR head satisfies acceptance, safety and correctness requirements and associated validation passed. - Ordinary reporting path: coder/implementation Task produces changed files, a PR/commit and self-reported success. - Complementary access path: separately configured read-only reviewer Agent receives exact PR head SHA plus bounded changed-file/patch context; the controller rejects stale or malformed verdicts and separately verifies required validation-task provenance. - Independence boundary: dedicated reviewer Task/runtime and read-intent workspace are isolated from the implementation Task, forbidden from modifying the checkout or receiving write credentials. Reviewer model may be an external supported runtime, but Orka owns dispatch, evidence identity, ingestion and return. - Who acts on findings: RepositoryMonitor records needschanges/safety states as blocked readiness; coordinator or explicit orka:fix/orka:fix-ci repair tasks must produce a new exact-head receipt, reset stale review and re-review before readiness. Optional automerge enforces fresh review/CI and policy gates; merge never follows review text alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic self-report, untested diffs, stale review, hidden security/correctness defects, unsupported validation claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer provides an evidence-backed contrary verdict and the monitoring/workflow path can block advancement or require repair. - Decision owner: distinct reviewer Agent for substantive critique; first-party controller for trustworthy exact-head admission and current readiness state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only workspace, signed/scoped inventory, JSON verdict schema, versioned review records, CI and separate validation provenance, bounded repair jobs. - Closure path: submitted PR → exact-head reviewer evidence → accepted verdict/blocked state → corrective agent task or human disposition → new head and re-review → readiness/merge policy. - Boundary reachability: the packaged reviewer Agent and RepositoryMonitor CRD/controller are first-party runnable paths, not a bare example prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critique is a separate model-driven reviewer and its findings are returned through first-party readiness/repair feedback; deterministic SHA/CI checks enforce validity but do not generate the semantic review. - Evidence: RepositoryMonitor, controller, review result ingestion and repair. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct owner evidenced that interprets changing external prospects and selects longer-horizon adaptation of the Orka operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider changes, future project requirements and workflow evolution were checked as plausible paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no source-backed right to convert trend/forecast/strategy intelligence into a revised ongoing mission capacity/plan, distinct from present PR repair. - Decision owner: users/administrators preconfigure Agents, providers, schedules and policies; individual coding tasks may modify repositories but that is mission S1, not system-level S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduled monitors, task templates, runtime-policy config, CI and dashboards. - Closure path: recurring current-state review and operational corrections are evidenced, but not future-looking intelligence → adaptation choice → changed S3 capability → later effectiveness feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repeated runs, CI repair, model configuration and repository improvement do not alone prove a distinct prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: README; chatsystemprompt.go; RepositoryMonitor guidance. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary unresolved ultimate-identity/policy adjudication and return process is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting goals, namespace trust and provider/tool allowances were considered but are preselected operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for an independent identity-policy authority beyond fixed platform administration and task-scoped approvals. - Decision owner: cluster administrators and user/tenant policy configuration; automated admission/RBAC only enforce already chosen constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kubernetes RBAC/admission, namespace authorization, scoped context tokens and provider/model allowlists. - Closure path: operator settings constrain later pods; no open organization identity-policy conflict → legitimate identity decision → authoritative return loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project governance, role prompts, admission policy and security rules do not create a semantic S5 organ. - Evidence: README; security documentation; ToolExecutor authorization. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200/assessments/or/orka.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 10b55d8799d18dedfb18f8aaa4b1c20fd604d200\">10b55d8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:27:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHub</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hawkingrei/agenthub/tree/7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f1f0450ebb58b3cb77b75c9669b4ad1ab8309f7\">7f1f045 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Workers deliver assigned engineering outcomes with local initiative and repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementation/test failures, new findings and scoped blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select local investigation/actions/repair and report evidence rather than wait for coordinator micro-approval. - Decision owner: Worker agent under the shipped worker execution role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP provider bridge, role-skill installation, task records, workspace tools and evidence stores. - Closure path: Task assignment/context reaches worker → agent acts → workerstatus/artifact pointers reach coordinator → accepted review or concrete change request drives continued work. - Boundary reachability: Team creation selects compiled worker prompt and role skill profile; ACP attaches mandatory managed skills before execution. External CLI is execution support, not an unexamined donor of the role’s rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker prompt explicitly gives initiative within its lane. Removing the agent leaves transport/task state without the same implementation decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; crates/agenthub-team-prompts/prompts/defaultteamworkerprompt.txt; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent competing agents from simultaneously owning the same actionable mailbox topic and reconcile ownership/dependency conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers can claim one topic or conflicting assignments; claim rejection identifies the active owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses claim, watch/wait, release or peer/coordinator adjustment after concrete ownership feedback. - Decision owner: Participating Team agents; coordinator resolves cross-worker ambiguity when needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite topic claim/lease transaction, stable topic keys, mailbox error/status feedback and shipped mailbox discipline. - Closure path: A live conflicting claim returns ThreadClaimConflict/owner; losing agent switches to watch/wait rather than executing as a second owner; release/expiry enables later accepted work. - Boundary reachability: Both worker/coordinator ACP roles require the actor-mailbox skill. The shipped actor triage path invokes the same claim transaction and exposes its concrete conflict recovery; custom coordination code is not required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SQL exclusion only enforces the selected claim. The mandatory agent mailbox contract supplies the behavior-changing triage response; a bare mailbox would not suffice. - Evidence: skills/team/team-actor-mailbox.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/mailboxthreads.rs; src/team/manager/mailboxerrors.rs; crates/agenthub-acp/src/teamroleskills.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Distinct S1 units: separate worker agents with assigned outcomes and local implementation discretion in the same Team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing active ownership of one topic/task-related thread, not merely two agents exchanging messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim ownership plus agent watch/wait/release and explicit handoff. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict response names owner and instructs loser to watch/release/wait, withholding duplicate execution until ownership permits it. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source implements and the installed skill handles an actual exclusive-ownership collision, beyond message delivery.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain cohesion of current Team work through explicit ownership, priority, accountability and intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/duplicated tasks, blocked workers, ownership mismatch, competing priorities and unsupported completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise canonical assignee/priority and current task commitments, park blocked work, accept/reopen based on evidence. - Decision owner: Coordinator agent in the installed Team mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor task create/list/update tools, task journals, execution context, mailbox evidence, role/permission gates and runtime views. - Closure path: Coordinator reads current task/member evidence → prioritizes or reassigns/changes state with journal reason → persisted canonical task/actor message changes worker’s next lane → returned evidence is reconciled again. - Boundary reachability: Compiled coordinator prompt and mandatory role skills explicitly route to task governance/lifecycle; supported actor/API tools persist chosen priorities and assignees. No adjacent contributor-management agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This extends beyond decomposing one prompt: the role deliberately reconciles ongoing task truth, stale duplicates, ownership drift, waiting commitments and evidence freshness. Runtime SQL/permissions execute rather than choose these decisions. - Evidence: skills/team/team-coordinator-orchestrator.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/manager/taskcatalog.rs; src/team/manager/taskupdates.rs; src/api/teams.rs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Source-based boundary judgment; no live model or production effectiveness claim. - Whole-system current view: Team task set/Kanban, member identity cards and checkpoints, blockers, notes and mailbox evidence. - Current-control decision scope: owner/priority/replanning, task commitment/acceptance and block/unblock intervention across the current Team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No additional materially independent complementary operational-audit path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading execution or management claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately authoritative audit judgment and corrective control return. - Decision owner: Coordinator reviews ordinary worker evidence; permission reviewer decides narrower execution permission. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP event history, task review, permissions and developer prompt-review plugin. - Closure path: Task inreview returns requested changes to worker, but this is the normal delivery/acceptance path; no separate complementary organizational-audit mode is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Architect/reviewer naming and raw logs alone do not turn normal coordinator acceptance into S3. - Evidence: skills/team/team-task-lifecycle.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs; src/team/permissionreview/selection.rs; plugins/agenthub-prompt-engineering/skills/agenthub-team-prompt-review/SKILL.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability/environment change beyond current task learning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Develop/admit prospective organizational capability options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: Agents retain project lessons and may patch their own profile; user controls broader product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace memory, profilepatchproposal, timed follow-ups and runtime recovery spine. - Closure path: Selected notes/profile changes inform later tasks, without an established external/future option-to-capability regulatory conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-profile drift repair and durable lessons do not alone establish S4; planned Mem/activation design cannot supply current closure. - Evidence: docs/features/team-workspace-memory-contract.md; src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/team-worker-executor.SKILL.md; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate-policy/identity adjudication loop at Team recursion established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Purpose/ultimate-policy tension, beyond member identity metadata and execution risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Return an authoritative ultimate-purpose/policy decision into subsequent Team operation. - Decision owner: User configures Team; agents own only scoped self-profile proposals and operational commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent cards, role skill restrictions, profile patches and permission approvals. - Closure path: Description/prompt changes update local role metadata; ACP approvals permit scoped commands. Neither is an evidenced ultimate-policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The word identity in Agent Cards does not make role-metadata editing S5. - Evidence: src/api/teams.rs; skills/team/TEAMAGENTS.md; skills/team/team-task-governance.SKILL.md; src/team/permissionreview/dispatcher.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a/assessments/ag/agenthub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 16805b7f353f8cb58b1aafb55c1c3f20ef01076a\">16805b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T22:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chorus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Chorus-AIDLC/Chorus/tree/148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148b7928b99773f7fbdf61080940bd6995b06934\">148b792 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded Chorus tasks and produce software changes/evidence under the task&#39;s acceptance criteria and project context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation choices, failures, tests, project constraints and task-specific ambiguity encountered while completing assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool/debugging actions needed to satisfy an assigned task and decide when work is ready to submit for verification. - Decision owner: the autonomous task worker agent launched under the shipped Chorus develop/worker protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task/proposal state, acceptance criteria, permissions, session/daemon dispatch, agent integration adapters, Chorus MCP tools and persisted work reports. - Closure path: orchestrator dispatches an unblocked task → worker reads Chorus context and executes autonomously in the target repository → worker reports work/self-check and submits for verification → resulting state/evidence returns to Chorus for audit/control. - Boundary reachability: Chorus ships the worker/develop skill contracts and supported adapters/daemon paths that launch autonomous coding-agent sessions as the standard operating surface; the external coding harness supplies execution capabilities but the Chorus project/task contract and return path are first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the task contract constrains scope, while the running worker agent chooses the substantive implementation sequence and tool actions rather than Chorus deterministically prescribing them. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo task execution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S1 autonomy is credited to the running task-agent role reachable through Chorus&#39;s first-party integration surfaces, not to deterministic task-state machinery or to undocumented behavior of any particular external coding harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated interference/oscillation among distinct S1 workers is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an S2 disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task DAG dependencies, unblocked-task queries, dependency-ordered waves, project/task state, comments and worker isolation organize and sequence work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 conflict/oscillation witness. - Closure path: dependency completion changes which tasks become runnable, but this is task sequencing/current control rather than evidence of a separate S2 attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple workers and a DAG are insufficient under the Profile; the reviewed evidence does not identify a concrete recurring interference between S1 units and a distinct coordination relation targeted at attenuating that interference. - Evidence: $yolo wave execution, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dependency ordering can prevent invalid execution order, but no separate S2-specific interference/attenuation relation is evidenced strongly enough to publish a positive state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a project-wide current operational view and decide which commitments start, wait, reopen, advance or escalate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete dependencies, failed or missing worker submissions, task-review failures, proposal-review failures, stuck work, exhausted review rounds and changing readiness across the task DAG. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next dependency-ready task wave, dispatch workers, decide how to handle non-ready/failed results, reopen failed tasks, advance verified tasks and stop/escalate when the project cannot progress. - Decision owner: the model-driven orchestrating agent executing the first-party $yolo workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent project/proposal/task state, chorusgetunblockedtasks, status transitions, acceptance criteria, admin transition APIs, reviewer comments, permissions and daemon/session lifecycle. - Closure path: orchestrator reads current project/task/review state → chooses wave/advance/reopen/escalate action → Chorus state/API transitions and worker dispatch implement it → changed task states alter subsequent readiness and execution → refreshed project state returns to the orchestrator. - Boundary reachability: $yolo is a shipped first-party Chorus skill and explicitly provides a complete autonomous mode using Chorus MCP/project state; its current-control decisions are not borrowed from repository CI or human project administration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-state and dependency code enforce constraints, but the orchestrating agent chooses the current portfolio action, handles exceptional results and decides whether to continue, rework or escalate. - Whole-system current view: project, proposal, task DAG, unblocked tasks, task statuses, review comments/verdicts and completion state across the Chorus project. - Current-control decision scope: current worker commitments, wave dispatch, rework/reopen, progression after review, and escalation when progress conditions cannot be satisfied. - Evidence: plugins/chorus/skills/yolo/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: server-side validation, permission checks and dependency computation are enforcement/support. S3 credit comes from the orchestrating agent&#39;s evidenced project-wide current-control choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge proposal and implementation completion claims through separate read-only reviewer agents and return blocking findings into corrective project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing requirements, weak acceptance criteria, dependency/design errors, implementation drift, circular or inadequate self-tests, build/test failure and developer self-verification blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect evidence independently, classify findings as blocking/non-blocking and issue the current round&#39;s VERDICT that determines whether the orchestrator advances or returns the work for correction. - Decision owner: the separate proposal-reviewer or task-reviewer agent for the corresponding audit stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer permissions, proposal/task/Idea/comment APIs, filesystem/test/build inspection, structured verdict format, persisted comments, review-round limits and admin state-transition APIs. - Closure path: producer submits proposal/task → distinct reviewer gathers complementary evidence and posts current-round verdict → orchestrator reads that verdict → FAIL causes reject/revise/resubmit or reopen/rework → the corrected artifact is reviewed again before advancement. - Boundary reachability: both reviewer definitions and the orchestration that invokes/consumes them are shipped first-party Chorus plugin surfaces in the standard autonomous workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers make substantive adversarial judgments from separate evidence and are prevented from editing the work under review; deterministic state transitions only enforce the downstream response selected from the verdict. - Claim being audited: proposal completeness/alignment before implementation and task implementation/acceptance correctness before verification/completion. - Ordinary reporting path: PM/developer agents create proposal drafts or implementation, self-check/report work and submit it through ordinary Chorus state transitions. - Complementary access path: reviewer agents separately fetch originating Idea/proposal/task/AC/comments and inspect project files, diffs, tests/builds or proposal dependency coverage under read-only constraints. - Independence boundary: reviewer roles are separate spawned agents with explicit read-only restrictions and distinct reviewer contracts; they do not author the proposal/code they evaluate during the review pass. - Who acts on findings: the $yolo orchestrator reads the current verdict and rejects/reopens or approves/verifies accordingly, causing the affected planning/development agents to perform subsequent correction. - Evidence: proposal reviewer, task reviewer, $yolo review loops. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer verdict strings are not credited merely because they are called review; the positive state rests on separate evidence access, read-only independence and an explicit findings-to-rework closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project search, Idea elaboration, proposal generation, repository/context inspection, persistent reports and reviewer feedback improve current project execution but do not establish prospective environment sensing that changes the harness/project&#39;s future capability program. - Closure path: current-project information feeds current planning and rework; no separate future-facing adaptation selection/return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks inside the current product request and learning from current review are not sufficient to map S4 without an environment-facing prospective adaptation loop. - Evidence: $yolo planning/review workflow, README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Chorus can be extended and its agents can research externally, but generic agent capability or project planning does not establish S4 ownership for the harness at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the Chorus-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user prompt/Idea intent, API-key permissions, admin approval/verification rights, project settings and review-round limits constrain operation but are operational/task policy rather than an evidenced S5 identity loop. - Closure path: permissions and approvals affect allowed current actions, but no disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to an authoritative S5 actor and returned as governing identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user origin, admin permission or approval gate is not automatically S5; the reviewed project boundary does not expose an identity-level policy negotiation/closure path. - Evidence: README.md, $yolo prerequisite/admin flow. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: humans can operate/administer Chorus and configure permissions, but those facts alone do not establish a parent-governed S5 function under Methodology 0.3.5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f/assessments/chorus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f\">60fa7f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sd0x-dev-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sd0xdev/sd0x-harness/tree/04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 04e8a5e3f5d9c482937c2ad2170fab5fbd02b630\">04e8a5e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify a requested software change or investigation in a user project through model-directed project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar project context, failing tests, faulty edits, changed requirements and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, tests, delegated specialist assistance and repairs in response to evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude Code engineering actor using the installed first-party project rules and specialist skills; the external Claude host executes tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin rules, installed project setup, hooks, review-state digest, git guards and verification scripts. - Closure path: user task → actor work and tools → test/review evidence or failure → actor correction/re-verification → project changes and report. - Boundary reachability: the documented full Claude plugin mode installs skills/rules/hooks and the session imports them; unlike skills-only mode, the full mode supplies the completion and review path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the actor selects engineering actions, whereas scripts record and gate outcomes without choosing code changes; external host internals are not credited as first-party decisions. - Evidence: installation and ownership model, active session rules, project setup and installed surfaces, hook wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: source demonstrates a supported installed mode, not deployment frequency or measured task success; first-party credit is for the composed plugin mode, not the external host&#39;s general autonomous capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference regulation is established for the assessed standard coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: read-only fanout and specialist handoffs are present, but no concrete conflicting changes between autonomous S1 work cells are regulated by a specified returned coordination decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: not established; planner/executor role dispatch and report ordering do not themselves decide collision attenuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: report-only workflow planning, bounded read-only fanout and sequential specialist skill calls. - Closure path: workflow reports return to the caller; no evidenced change to later competing S1 behaviour from conflict regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven orchestration of independent read-only findings alone is not S2. - Evidence: read-only orchestrate contract, single reviewer default, specialist definition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other user-composed multi-agent modes are possible but are not credited without a shipped interference-specific return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control relation is established at the configured project-session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk severity, code/doc dirtiness and review failures are constrained by local quality gates, not shown as current resource bargaining across operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: risk tier and gate obligations operate on task/change evidence without an evidenced whole-system allocation or corrective control decision. - Decision owner: model and user choose execution/review details within static policy; digest-state scripts enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anchor Register, tier selection, review slots, hook reminders, scoped guards and human exits. - Closure path: review failures cause local work/review retries; no independently evidenced S3 current-control feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent-owned local repair and deterministic completion invariant do not themselves establish S3. - Evidence: review tiers and loop, policy tiers and ownership, digest-bound state, nonblocking hook. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: risk-scaled verification is important but cannot substitute for a whole-system current view.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an implementer&#39;s completion/correctness claim by inspecting actual changed project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producer may falsely declare completion or miss faults not visible in its own summary/checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: give a separate severity-scoped audit verdict from raw code/diff access and determine whether corrective work is owed. - Decision owner: the separately dispatched Codex reviewer model makes the audit judgment; the producing Claude actor owns subsequent repairs; no digest parser is credited as auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only codex exec transport, frozen changed-file metadata, review-state verdict slot, risk thresholds and fallback validation. - Closure path: after code changes, /codex-review-fast → independent reviewer source investigation → Ready/Blocked findings → producing actor fixes blocking issues → re-review → fresh pass records a digest-bound slot required before completion. - Boundary reachability: the full plugin installs the review skill, transport and model-facing auto-loop obligations, and hooks remind after edits; the review is a documented first-party supported optional tool path, not a CI-only or developer-dogfood audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model agent reads the actual diff/source and decides findings; transport, severity parser and receipt record do not substitute for its judgment. - Claim being audited: project change is correct enough under the configured risk tier to be complete. - Ordinary reporting path: producer&#39;s change summary, tests and its own verification. - Complementary access path: separate Codex sandbox source/diff inspection and independent review prompt; fallback reviewers are controlled by recorded transport failure semantics. - Independence boundary: read-only external Codex session receives frozen task and changed-file metadata but reads source itself; it is not the producer&#39;s self-verification. Both hosted tools remain outside first-party implementation ownership. - Who acts on findings: producing Claude actor performs scoped fixes and re-dispatches; human is reserved for explicitly enumerated exits. - Evidence: independent source review and fix loop, severity/scope gate, separate reviewer transport, repeat-until-current-pass contract, digest-bound return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: evidence is shipped protocol/code, not a captured successful live independent audit; review may be blocked by missing external Codex credentials or human exits in particular installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt subsequent project-engineering guidance from recurring external/user corrections. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user-observed failures whose recurrence is not prevented by current rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinguish a reusable error pattern and, after repetition, update project-local operational guidance for future sessions. - Decision owner: the model-driven engineering actor following the first-party self-improvement rule; the user can originate corrections and decide version-control inclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: namespaced lesson log, repeated-pattern criterion, rule-file update and normal doc review. - Closure path: user correction on a recurrent failure → actor writes a structured lesson with prevention signal → pattern reaches promotion criterion → actor promotes guidance to project rules → future sessions read updated rules and act differently. - Boundary reachability: the published self-improvement rule is installed via standard full-plugin project setup and imported by session guidance; the namespaced lesson file is provisioned on first occurrence and later local rules are loaded. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model must recognize the pattern, select the prevention and write/publish the operational rule; file persistence alone is not the adaptation judgment. - External distinction: user correction provides external feedback about unmet project expectations, not solely internal planning. - Future / prospective distinction: lesson/rule promotion addresses future recurrence across tasks and sessions. - Adaptation option generated: prevention rule and detection signal reusable for later work. - Path back into current capability / S3: project-installed rules govern later Claude sessions, closing adaptation into operational capability; no distinct S3 is claimed. - Evidence: correction-to-rule procedure, rule installation, project rule imports. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: contingent on actual recurring correction and session-level rule promotion; source does not establish measured improvement or autonomous change to the plugin&#39;s published universal anchors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is established in the supported project engineering session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local configuration, security anchors, scope requests and approval/merge risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-boundary identity-level decision is returned through an established S5 channel. - Decision owner: developer and user set framework identity and non-negotiable rules; model may deviate from defaults only inside those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Anchor Register, project overrides, user confirmation and protected git/review gates. - Closure path: local tasks use preselected rules; no evidenced policy-level proposal → legitimate parent judgment → returned change to the session&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static constraint hierarchy and user approval of ordinary actions do not create S5 closure. - Evidence: Anchor/Default boundaries, fixed security policy, scope and developer role. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: project maintainers can change the published plugin policy in a different recursion, which is explicitly excluded here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee/assessments/sd/sd0x-dev-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d6959de776c5df5b41ae7fb77c94cfd9bd6236ee\">d6959de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T21:37:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Your Claude Engineer</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/your-claude-engineer/tree/e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e7c064ac915cfeb29496c558795efa125a121863\">e7c064a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement a selected app feature through model-selected coding actions, browser tests and correction from tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete feature requirements, changing code context, failing browser flows, runtime errors and interrupted long-running model sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model chooses concrete edits, commands, browser inspections and repairs from the orchestrator&#39;s issue context and observed tool outcomes. - Decision owner: model-backed coding subagent executed through Claude Agent SDK; the first-party harness supplies role/prompt/tool configuration and continuity, not the SDK model&#39;s entire general capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated project directory, first-party subagent definitions, Arcade tools, sandbox, bash pretool hook and retry/session boundaries. - Closure path: app specification and Linear issue → orchestrator delegates detailed work → coding model inspects/edits/tests → Playwright or tool result → fix and verification → implementation evidence returned to orchestrator/Linear. - Boundary reachability: autonomousagentdemo.py invokes the first-party run loop; client.py wires AGENTDEFINITIONS and built-in/Playwright tools into the SDK client; coding prompt directs real code/test actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation and repair actions depend on model interpretation of evidence; the shell allowlist, iteration delay and state markers cannot make those decisions alone. - Evidence: entry/session loop, SDK client and tool wiring, specialized coding definition, coding prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a live provider/Arcade/Playwright session was not run; SDK authentication and third-party success remain outside the evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct mechanism is shown for attenuating a concrete disturbance between independently viable, competing operational S1 coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear, coding, GitHub and Slack agents exchange messages and roles, but no competing source-changing S1 claims or resource-contention disturbance is evidenced in the default sequential one-issue flow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific allocation, negotiated conflict-resolution or revised reciprocal S1 commitments. - Decision owner: none shown; orchestrator transfers context and orders specialized tasks, while SDK Task dispatch executes bounded child work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation, Linear statuses, one-issue sequencing, local generated-project git and task handoff summaries. - Closure path: orchestrator orders a single implementation issue and passes results to Linear/GitHub/Slack; this is cross-role task routing, not a source-backed interference-damping return between contending operational cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model-chosen next issue belongs to whole-current S3 at this recursion; it does not itself close S2 competition among independent parallel work units. - Evidence: explicit one-issue-at-a-time rule and context transfer, sequential feature workflow, role definitions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external SDK may support parallel Task calls, but this source does not demonstrate a distinct concrete S2 attenuation path for the shipped project workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current whole project backlog, work priority and resumed progress across many feature issues/sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale issue status, wrong next-work choice, incomplete previously Done features, session context resets and missing acceptance evidence. - Whole-system current view: the model orchestrator requests Linear META context, counts across Done/In Progress/Todo and full details of the next issue, and reads the generated project&#39;s current state before deciding its continuation. - Current-control decision scope: model selects whether to stop and repair regression or proceed to the next feature, delegates the issue and decides when to mark it Done based on required browser evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which pending portfolio issue should be worked next and overturn current progress when verification fails; issue transitions/continuation return to Linear and next fresh session. - Decision owner: first-party prompted model orchestrator with live Linear agent and coding-agent feedback; neither static CLI loop nor Linear SaaS is credited with the model&#39;s project-wide decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: META issue, .linearproject.json continuation marker, project progress summary, subagent tools, status counts, completion signal. - Closure path: fresh session reads project/Linear whole-current status → orchestrator asks for retest → if failed directs correction, else chooses next issue → dispatches code and evidence → updates Linear Done/next state → next session refreshes the picture. - Boundary reachability: the active client loads orchestratorprompt.md and the iteration loop recreates a client per session, so this is not an unused design diagram. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prompt gives the model discretion to choose/reprioritize actual current work and act on regression findings; the deterministic loop only reopens sessions and recognizes the completion token. - Evidence: orchestrator continuation and priority/verification steps, whole-project completion decision, fresh-session state and rerun, live prompt registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the whole here is the one generated app&#39;s issue portfolio, not all of an enterprise&#39;s projects; the Linear account&#39;s actual data/permissions were not accessed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test prior completed-feature claims against actual running UI/interaction evidence before the S3 orchestrator takes new work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear may say Done while a previously working feature has regressed; summary or screenshot receipts can be stale or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven coding subagent runs a fresh browser verification for 1–2 completed features and can return FAIL plus screenshots; orchestrator must suspend new work and request regression fixing. - Decision owner: separately invoked coding verifier model for direct Playwright observations; orchestrator owns current feature plan and the fix order. This is the same coding role as producers, but a distinct subsequent Task invocation, not a third-party auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory pre-work verification gate, browser navigation/click/snapshot/screenshot tools, structured PASS/FAIL report, no-Done-without-screenshot rule. - Closure path: prior Linear Done claim → new continuation requests browser retest → independent current app UI observations → FAIL evidence → orchestrator orders fix and re-test before new task → updated issue/current project status. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator prompt makes retest mandatory on every continuation/new feature; coding subagent prompt defines a dedicated Verification Test task; Playwright tools are registered in the normal SDK client. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model verifier decides whether the live UI corroborates the prior completion account; neither the prior producer&#39;s summary nor a deterministic status parser supplies this judgment. - Claim being audited: previously marked-Done project features still work and are fit to leave closed. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear Done status, prior implementation/test summary and saved project/meta issue context. - Complementary access path: fresh coding verifier goes to running app via Playwright, exercises 1–2 completed features, observes screenshots/browser state and reports PASS/FAIL. - Independence boundary: verifier is a fresh delegated Task/subagent context relative to prior iteration and S3 issue status, but reuses the &#39;coding&#39; role/provider and is not organizationally independent; that narrower evidence-based independence is the claim credited. - Who acts on findings: S3 orchestrator blocks next feature and asks coding agent to repair a reproduced regression; verification repeats before onward progress. - Evidence: mandatory verification and remedial gate, continuation&#39;s direct retest and failure return, coding agent browser verification protocol, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: prompts specify behavior; no captured live regression was reproduced in this review. The same coding agent role is reused, so independence is bounded to fresh contextual sensing versus S3/earlier Done account, not external independent peer review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped organizational loop selects durable prospective changes to the harness&#39;s future capabilities from external environmental intelligence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user-specified app goals and task regressions are operations/current control, not an ongoing externally informed future adaptation mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none beyond issue-by-issue project implementation and user-edited prompts/spec/config. - Decision owner: user supplies app specification and permission settings; orchestrator chooses present feature priority but does not autonomously revise the harness&#39;s strategic operating design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: appspec.txt, developer-editable prompt files, Linear backlog, META continuation memory and model configuration flags. - Closure path: spec/backlog → present coding and feature selection; no external change signal → strategic option → selected lasting future operating-capability shift is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: long-running context and future work items are ordinary S1/S3 continuity, not independent S4 environmental search/adaptation. - External distinction: third-party project requirements and Linear status are task inputs, not autonomous environmental foresight. - Future / prospective distinction: an unfinished backlog is a plan for task execution, not adaptation of the executing organization&#39;s future structure. - Evidence: project specification and prompt customization, current issue workflow, session continuity. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can change the prompts or request a different application; that is external reconfiguration, not a built-in strategic return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous ultimate-policy or legitimate organizational identity owner closes an S5 constitutional return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permission, command safety, OAuth authorization and product scope are externally pre-set restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for determining the project&#39;s ultimate legitimate purpose or amendments to binding identity/policy. - Decision owner: project owner, external credentials administrator and fixed script/security policies govern these matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK sandbox, file tool allowlist, bash PreToolUse hook, Arcade OAuth, fixed appspec and allowed-command list. - Closure path: configuration constrains tool execution; no first-party deliberation over constitutional goals and downward reauthorization is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement of static user/configuration rights is not policy-identity formation. - Evidence: security and permission construction, SDK tool/hook invocation, first-party bash guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within reviewed boundary. - Caveats: external user/organization policy is excluded from this system in focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7/assessments/yo/your-claude-engineer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 21315010a2fe15de7f6fd1c8c001cbedcbb865f7\">2131501 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T18:54:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Everything Claude Code (ECC)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/affaan-m/ECC/tree/2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b6e839771e53096d8451a213d40dc64ec8acac0\">2b6e839 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute assigned project work inside ECC-managed model-driven sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, tool/test observations, failures, intermediate artifacts and uncertainty about the next substantive action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation/tool actions and iterate from observations until the assigned task completes or terminates. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent session launched/managed by ECC. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ECC2 lifecycle/store, worktrees, process launch, session identity, output capture, stop/resume and NanoClaw persistence where used. - Closure path: assigned task → ECC-managed model session → model-selected actions → repository/tool observations → further model actions → session result/state. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 start and the session manager directly launch supported model-driven agent sessions; NanoClaw is also shipped first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC lifecycle code manages the process but does not choose its substantive next coding/tool action. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, scripts/claw.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the launched host harness retains its own internal tool-loop implementation; its S2–S5 semantics are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete collision risk among parallel agent S1 units working on overlapping/coupled repository state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: same-file/line overlap and dependency/tree coupling that can make concurrent changes interfere. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compute right-of-way and an S2-specific hold/steer advisory; a return consumer must still apply that maneuver to the affected S1. - Decision owner: no autonomous owner is closed over the full loop; current right-of-way is deterministic and the agent-return actor is absent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: working-set extraction, dependency graph, overlap/dependency/tree risk channels, priority calculation, advisory feed and worktree isolation. - Closure path: parallel worksets → collision-risk calculation → hold/steer advisory → constructor gap at the return leg into S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: the scanner and advisory/event contract are shipped first-party paths specifically designed around inter-agent collision; the missing TCAS hook prevents A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic right-of-way calculation is not autonomous organizational ownership; ECC exposes the S2-specific path but not its autonomous closure. - Evidence: docs/design/agent-proximity.md, docs/control-plane/VIEW-CONTRACT.md, docs/control-plane/TCAS-HOOK.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: TCAS-HOOK.md explicitly marks the return hook design-only; older design prose must not be read as proof that steering is already wired. - Distinct S1 units: separate ECC-managed model-driven sessions/worktrees acting concurrently on the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits and dependency-coupled changes create concrete collision/merge risk between those sessions. - Attenuating coordination relation: ECC proximity computes pairwise risk, priority/right-of-way and a hold/steer maneuver advisory. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ECC exposes the action-bearing advisory as the S2-specific return primitive, but the consumer that applies it before an edit remains to be composed; this is the constructor gap. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation exists specifically to reduce measured cross-agent code collision, with explicit pairwise risk and maneuver semantics rather than merely moving messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current team/session commitments, allocation, backlog pressure, intervention and worktree integration across an ECC-managed project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing active/stopped sessions, unread handoffs, idle/saturated delegates, backlog pressure, worktree readiness and current need to add/reuse/rebalance/stop capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose delegation/assignment/rebalance/stop/resume/merge actions from current team state. - Decision owner: constructor mode — autonomous owner must be composed; parent mode — the human operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/message store, dashboard/team/status, delegate-selection scoring, backlog counts, worktree state and daemon/process lifecycle. - Closure path: current team/backlog/worktree view → operator or composed controller chooses intervention → ECC executes it → commitments/state change → refreshed view reflects the result. - Boundary reachability: ECC2 ships Delegate/Assign/AutoDispatch/CoordinateBacklog/Rebalance/Stop/Merge surfaces and current team/status views. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic assignment/maintenance can execute policy but does not constitute a model-owned whole-team discretionary judgment; the operator path is separately closed. - Evidence: ecc2/src/main.rs, ecc2/src/session/manager.rs, skills/team-agent-orchestration/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ECC2 is explicitly alpha; roadmap-only autonomous orchestration is not credited. - Whole-system current view: dashboard/session/team/coordination-status surfaces expose active sessions, delegate relationships, backlog/current state and worktree information at the project recursion. - Current-control decision scope: delegation/reuse/spawn, backlog redistribution, stop/resume, team intervention and worktree merge/integration.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit a candidate using complementary reviewer actors and return defects into corrective production work before shipping. - Disturbance / variety regulated: semantic defects, hallucinations, incompleteness, compliance violations and technical errors the producer may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: two isolated reviewers independently issue structured PASS/FAIL judgments; any failure blocks shipping and supplies issues to a fix cycle followed by fresh reviewers. - Decision owner: the two independent reviewer agents for audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: identical rubric/input contract, context isolation, structured verdicts, deterministic both-pass gate, bounded iterations and escalation. - Closure path: candidate → independent reviewers → findings → both-pass gate or fix issue set → revised candidate → fresh reviewers → ship or bounded escalation. - Boundary reachability: Santa Method is a shipped ECC skill intended for supported hosts with subagent capability and specifies the full reviewer/correction topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic audit judgments come from separate model actors; deterministic code only aggregates their verdicts. - Evidence: skills/santa-method/SKILL.md, commands/santa-loop.md. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strongest independence requires a supported host&#39;s separate subagent contexts; the documented inline fallback is weaker and is not the basis for A. - Claim being audited: the produced candidate&#39;s correctness/completeness/compliance against the original specification and rubric. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent&#39;s own output and deterministic build/test checks remain the normal production evidence. - Complementary access path: two fresh reviewer agents independently receive the same original specification, candidate and rubric outside the producer&#39;s context. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separate agent contexts/processes and do not share each other&#39;s assessments; fresh reviewer instances are required after fixes. - Who acts on findings: a fix/production agent receives the merged critical issues, revises the candidate and submits it to fresh review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn operational experience and corrections into persistent learned behaviors that alter later ECC-hosted sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user corrections, error-resolution patterns, repeated workflows and project-specific versus general behavioral distinctions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — background Observer identifies pattern/content/scope and updates an instinct; parent mode — user selects a project instinct for promotion to global scope. - Decision owner: base mode — Observer/Haiku agent; parent mode — human user for explicit promotion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-use observation hooks, project detection, observation logs, confidence scoring, observer schedule, instinct stores and SessionStart injection. - Closure path: operational evidence → observer pattern judgment → persistent instinct → later SessionStart injection → subsequent agent behavior changes; parent promotion can widen a selected instinct&#39;s future scope. - Boundary reachability: continuous-learning-v2 ships the hooks, observer agent/loop, instinct store/CLI and SessionStart injection; the background mode is explicitly supported when enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic capture/thresholds support the loop, while the observer model decides the learned pattern; the learned result is then automatically returned into later context. - Evidence: skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/agents/observer.md, scripts/hooks/session-start.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: observer defaults to disabled and is not viable as a persistent background process on native Windows; the A claim is for the supported enabled mode. - External distinction: user corrections, observed failures/resolutions and repeated workflows/tool preferences captured from real prior sessions. - Future / prospective distinction: whether those observed patterns should become reusable behavior in subsequent sessions/projects rather than only solve the current task. - Adaptation option generated: evidence-backed project/global instincts with a trigger, action, scope and confidence; related instincts can additionally be evolved/promoted. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying instincts are automatically injected at later SessionStart, changing the behavioral context available to future S1 work; parent promotion can widen their scope.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed ECC project/runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/constitutional disturbance is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: consent/safety boundaries, profiles, permissions, rules, security hooks, governance capture, learned preferences and operator configuration. - Closure path: no standard path is established from a genuine identity/ultimate-policy matter through authoritative S5 judgment back into governing whole-system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ECC&#39;s policy surfaces regulate tasks/tools/review/security but do not establish an identity/constitutional authority organ at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, skills/autonomous-agent-harness/SKILL.md, skills/continuous-learning-v2/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream organization can impose an identity/constitution using ECC primitives; that separate system requires its own assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3/assessments/ecc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit feba4db663392fe60ec2b4e2f4f6633748c0c1a3\">feba4db &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:23:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/tree/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2\">fdba1b1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform code, test and project changes using an actual OpenCode backend agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source, ambiguous instructions, failing tests and tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which files to inspect/edit, what tests to run and what fix satisfies the task. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander working directly or separately invoked Worker; plugin only routes tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode host, tool adapters, session registry, mission memory and verification checklist. - Closure path: user task → Commander/Worker model tool actions → code/tests/evidence → reviewed mission result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves prompt/routing code but no task-specific implementation choice. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/worker.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: shipped model agent definitions and runtime delegation path. - Confidence: high for supported mode; provider performance unmeasured. - Caveats: standalone question-only runs do not exhibit a coding operational cell. - Boundary reachability: published OpenCode 2 plugin adds Commander; Worker is a supported optional delegate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no concrete first-party cross-operational conflict resolution circuit was established for parallel workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent worker file edits or interface changes may be incompatible when merged. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander assigns scopes/dependencies and can review results; no specific inter-S1 clash signal/repair relation shown. - Decision owner: Commander for current task delegation rather than a demonstrated S2-specific conflict decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel task manager, agentConcurrency, task result tool, scoped worker prompts and session IDs. - Closure path: workers report results to Commander but a collision-specific balancing signal and returned repair are not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: file ownership guidance and queue limits prevent some overlap but delegation alone is not specific inter-S1 oscillation regulation. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/agents/manager.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/tools/parallel/delegate-task.ts - Basis: bounded negative on parallel task lifecycle and Commander scope/dep guidance. - Confidence: medium; a real Commander could notice conflicts not proven as a repeatable shipped circuit. - Caveats: S3 whole-mission corrective work and S3 Reviewer findings are not automatically S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: direct a whole currently active project mission, allocate scoped model work and intervene when delegated commitments or verification stall. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished TODO leaves, dependencies, failed delegates, stagnant iterations and incomplete verification of overall goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commander selects task decomposition, model delegation, dependency ordering, replanning, repair or final acceptance. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander with whole-mission goal and result view, not the mission-loop continuation hook. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission TODO/status/sync issues, parallel task list/results, progress/stagnation guard, checklist and durable ledger. - Closure path: whole mission evidence and delegated status → Commander reassigns/replans or repairs → worker next action and updated TODO/verified completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses revised work and scope; deterministic retry/verification cannot produce design-specific corrective decisions. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop-handler.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts - Basis: first-party model role and continuing mission control feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for selected active mission mode. - Caveats: a trivial question or single direct edit has no multi-cell S3 demonstrated; budget/loop enforcement alone is not ownership. - Whole-system current view: Commander sees entire user goal, all mission TODO leaves, child task status/results and aggregate verification requirements. - Current-control decision scope: assign Workers/Planner/Reviewer, choose serial dependency order, take over blocked work, re-plan and decide evidence-backed completion. - Boundary reachability: normal Commander and /task mode with optional delegatetask, task-list and result tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Worker/Commander completion claim by checking actual project files and tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false success, broken interface contracts, untested regressions and incomplete TODO verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dedicated Reviewer model decides which checks to rerun and what findings block completion. - Decision owner: fresh role-specific Reviewer agent, not original implementer or deterministic checklist parser. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer prompt and scope, actual source/tests, mission checklist, sync-issues and returned task result. - Closure path: implementation result → Reviewer source/test checks → evidenced pass/fail/uncovered area → Commander repair or verified TODO completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer makes code-specific fault judgments rather than only checking a manifest flag. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/reviewer.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/prompts/common.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/verification.ts - Basis: supported optional role-separated reviewer and executable evidence-return contract. - Confidence: medium-high for configured review; no paid model E2E validation. - Caveats: optional delegation, possibly same provider and no external institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Worker or Commander claims an implementation is complete and matches acceptance checks. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer result, task status and self-reported tests. - Complementary access path: separate Reviewer reads actual affected files, interfaces and executes project checks independently. - Independence boundary: distinct Reviewer invocation and independent direct source/tests, not a separate organization. - Who acts on findings: Commander updates scope or repairs; Reviewer may mark only verified assigned checklist leaves and log integration issues. - Boundary reachability: published plugin optional Reviewer subagent available via delegatetask.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future external-environment adaptation loop into mission capability control was proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider changes, future market/user requirements and long-term technical alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner and Commander choose current technical steps, not enduring environmental adaptation options. - Decision owner: user/project leadership for long-horizon adaptation outside plugin. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner, mission memory, persistent TODO and model tools. - Closure path: current planning updates task implementation but no external future options negotiation with S3 is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an implementation plan or research tool call alone is not future-facing S4. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/subagents/planner.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/knowledge/mission-memory.ts - Basis: negative path review of Planner, mission continuation and memory. - Confidence: medium-high at package boundary. - Caveats: a user can prompt strategic research, which does not demonstrate shipped S4 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate identity-level policy decision loop is wired into the packaged OpenCode mission plugin. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission, mission replacement, project policy and final action boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human sets goals, stops or replaces missions, and approves operations as needed. - Decision owner: user/deployment parent for policy; plugin only enforces selected task permissions and state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stop/cancel commands, prompts, bounded concurrency, session replacement and verification gates. - Closure path: human can cancel or redirect a mission, but an identity-level exception is not routed to ultimate authority and returned as governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: system prompt constraints and user approvals over coding steps are not S5 identity closure. - Evidence: https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/agents/commander.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/src/core/loop/mission-loop.ts; https://github.com/agnusdei1207/opencode-orchestrator/blob/fdba1b1eea1e78d27aa733a421beaa61ad0bb8a2/README.md - Basis: bounded negative across commands, safety, mission replacement, prompts and user rights. - Confidence: medium-high for installed plugin. - Caveats: maintainer governance or an external company policy engine is outside the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea/assessments/op/opencode-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3ac4ecb7a0a5204143934e584720845a924425ea\">3ac4ecb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T00:24:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hive</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aden-hive/hive/tree/0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c387492067e8b7d3e1c803009169f202f30ed77\">0c38749 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: Queen/workers autonomously perform outcome-producing business-process work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the standard colony supplies agent-owned coordination through its shared ledger/plan, worker-report event path and Queen-driven convergence, providing concrete task-ownership and fleet-awareness mechanisms rather than simple one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the persistent Queen has a colony-wide current view through the plan/tracker and explicit authority to assign/fan out work, inspect and validate worker progress/results, converge execution and escalate exceptions. This is ongoing whole-colony current regulation rather than a component merely named manager.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Queen-side validation uses the ordinary lead/control path and the human Sentinel is an escalation mechanism; the reviewed standard distribution does not establish a distinct sufficiently independent autonomous complementary-audit path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflexion, memory and learned skills improve operation over time, but learning ≠ a distinct external/prospective intelligence function coupled to S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: Queen personas, behavior triggers, HITL and cost controls shape operation, but legitimate ultimate task/policy authority remains with the user/configuration rather than an agent-owned S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944/assessments/hive.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 961323b8cd7cc12dfe8f927e44f355c2116c9944\">961323b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">jcode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/1jehuang/jcode/tree/5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f33d6239b56b3d381d6b2c0e9b151eef0b54cbe\">5f33d62 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. jcode owns autonomous coding-agent sessions that execute repository work and can spawn/participate in swarm work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. The shared live plan carries explicit blockers, assignments and task state; worker outcomes and plan mutations change which nodes become runnable and which agent receives later work. Dependency and gate state therefore regulates inter-S1 ordering/conflict rather than merely routing messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The coordinator owns the current shared plan and worker population, assigns/spawns work, observes progress/heartbeats and reclaims stranded assignments when workers die. This supplies autonomous whole-swarm current regulation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Gate/verify node kinds and artifact checks occur inside the normal swarm execution path; no materially independent reviewer/auditor with distinct access and corrective authority was established at the pin.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Ambient Mode proactively inspects recent project activity and can launch useful maintenance work, while its memory learns from approvals/rejections. That is ongoing S1 work plus internal experiential memory, not a distinct outside-looking prospective loop that develops and feeds changed organizational capability into current regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The safety/approval architecture at the pin is explicitly a design document; moreover action permission by itself is not identity/ultimate-policy closure. No implemented S5 tension-resolution loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/jcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T22:03:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ante</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AntigmaLabs/ante/tree/74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 74ce24554341fb0c6cc3521baa5da5b94cedf881\">74ce245 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform user-directed software-engineering work through a repeated model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding requests, repository state, file/shell/tool observations, execution failures, model/context limits and delegated-work results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive next actions/tools/delegation and decide when enough operational evidence exists to return a result. - Decision owner: the running root model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon/session turn lifecycle, tool implementations, permission engine, provider transport, persistence, compaction and external/local inference hosts. - Closure path: user task → model decision → first-party tool/delegation execution → returned observation → subsequent model decision → completed task/result. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Ante TUI/headless/server paths instantiate this model/tool loop directly; no downstream harness composition is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime mechanisms execute and constrain actions, but the model owns the substantive selection of what to do next and whether the task is operationally complete. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/reference/architecture.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx, README headless/TUI usage. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference may be external or local; ownership is credited to the model actor operating through Ante&#39;s first-party loop, not to the provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate contention among concurrently delegated Ante work units for bounded shared inference capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents attempting concurrent model work when the selected local/model-serving environment can only process a smaller number of requests without queueing/timeouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine how much concurrent subagent work the session may admit before excess work waits. - Decision owner: constructor/operator configuration; Ante supplies the function-specific concurrency mechanism but no autonomous agent owns or revises the cap in the documented standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxconcurrentsubagents, waiting-for-slot queueing and parent interruption/cancellation behavior. - Closure path: parallel child calls → cap check → excess child waits → slot becomes available → queued child starts; later S1 execution therefore changes according to the coordination result. - Boundary reachability: the cap is a documented built-in session setting applied directly to built-in Agent calls in ordinary Ante execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses which subagents to delegate to, but the specific collision-attenuation policy is selected through settings and enforced by the runtime; an autonomous S2 decision owner is not supplied. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/subagents.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/reference/tools-reference.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic delegation is not credited as S2; only the documented shared-capacity interference and cap/queue relation are used. - Distinct S1 units: the root organization can launch multiple built-in/custom subagents that independently execute real delegated tasks with their own model context and tool set. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child model requests can exceed the usable concurrent capacity of the selected inference environment; the docs specifically call out a local model that handles one request at a time. - Attenuating coordination relation: maxconcurrentsubagents limits admitted parallel children and queues excess calls until a slot opens. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: queued child work does not begin until capacity becomes available, preventing the competing S1 requests from simultaneously consuming the bounded model-serving channel. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is explicitly tied to regulating a concrete concurrency/capacity interference among multiple operational child units, not to the existence of the Agent handoff itself.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current session&#39;s continuing commitment against a declared success condition and intervene when progress is insufficient or the goal is no longer reachable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete work, premature completion, current-turn outcomes that do not yet satisfy the session goal, and situations where the active commitment has become unreachable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after each turn, judge whether the whole-session goal is met, unreachable or requires another regulated continuation with corrective feedback. - Decision owner: the model-based goal evaluator in the first-party /goal mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal-session protocol state, deterministic continuation plumbing, session persistence and iteration cap. - Closure path: current conversation + goal → evaluator judgment → continue returns evaluator feedback into another operational turn, while met/unreachable terminates the commitment. - Boundary reachability: /goal is a shipped first-party TUI/protocol capability and can be used directly without a downstream controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the iteration cap is only a backstop; the substantive assessment of current performance and continue/stop state is made by the evaluator model and its feedback changes subsequent work. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/usage/goal-sessions.mdx, protocol documentation and changelog entries for goal evaluation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary one-turn execution alone would not establish S3; this state is credited from the supported goal-driven mode. - Whole-system current view: the goal evaluator receives the active success condition and evaluates the conversation after each completed turn rather than judging one isolated tool call. - Current-control decision scope: continue the current commitment with corrective feedback, or end it as met/unreachable; this regulates current performance/accountability for the session as a whole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge changed-code quality claims through multiple review lenses and return complementary findings for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplication, unnecessary complexity, avoidable inefficiency and misplaced abstraction that can survive the ordinary implementation/check path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each independent reviewer judges the same scoped changes under a distinct audit lens and returns supported findings without editing the work being audited. - Decision owner: constructor path; first-party curated simplify supplies the audit organization and reviewer contracts, but the standard Ante installation does not equip/invoke this lane automatically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skill discovery/equipping, parallel subagent calls, shared review scope, prohibition on reviewer edits, parent deduplication/validation and subsequent normal tool execution. - Closure path: implementation/diff → independent reviewer findings → parent validates/deduplicates → worthwhile corrections are applied and verified. - Boundary reachability: the skill is shipped in the public Ante repository and is installable through the documented first-party skill mechanism; it is therefore a concrete first-party constructor surface, not a hypothetical downstream design. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer judgments are agentic, but obtaining this S3 organization still requires the operator/developer to install/equip the curated skill; Ante does not supply it as a resident standard audit owner. - Evidence: curated/skills/simplify/SKILL.md, curated/README.md, docs-site/docs/extend/skills.mdx. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary tests, /diff, logs and root self-review are not credited; only the separate reviewer construction path supports C. - Claim being audited: that the current changed code is sufficiently reusable, simple, efficient and well-abstracted for the requested cleanup scope. - Ordinary reporting path: the root agent implements/inspects the task and runs its ordinary checks. - Complementary access path: independent subagents receive the same diff/scope under separate reuse, simplification, efficiency and abstraction lenses and return findings without modifying files. - Independence boundary: reviewer contexts are separate subagents and are prohibited from editing; the parent receives their findings only after each independent judgment is made. - Who acts on findings: the parent agent validates and deduplicates findings, applies worthwhile improvements and runs verification.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn reusable distinctions from user/project experience and convert them into persistent guidance that changes later sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, confirmations, project constraints, learned working patterns and memories that become stale or wrong over time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which observed lessons should be recorded, updated or removed as persistent project memory for future work. - Decision owner: the running Ante agent under the first-party auto-memory behavior. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-project memory directory, typed memory format, MEMORY.md cap, session-start memory injection and Write/Edit tools. - Closure path: current user/project interaction → agent identifies reusable lesson → memory is created/updated/removed → future session injects the retained memory into the system prompt → later operational choices change. - Boundary reachability: auto-memory is a documented built-in Ante capability enabled by default in interactive TUI sessions and optionally in headless mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user supplies environmental feedback, but the agent is explicitly taught to consult, record, revise and discard persistent memories; the runtime only stores and reloads the selected adaptation. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/extend/memory.mdx, preference/headless documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: session transcript persistence alone is not credited; the witness is the agent-selected conversion of experience into reusable future-session guidance. - External distinction: corrections, confirmations and project-specific guidance arrive from the user/project environment rather than from an internal fixed task plan alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the agent evaluates whether a lesson should guide later conversations and can remove or update it when no longer valid. - Adaptation option generated: persist or revise a concise typed memory/rule describing how future work should be handled. - Path back into current capability / S3: retained memories are automatically injected into later session prompts, changing the options and constraints available to subsequent S1 work and goal-regulated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established at the selected Ante boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator-defined prompts, permissions, profiles and project settings constrain behavior, but no specific identity/ultimate-policy conflict or proposal is routed through an S5 closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision path with complete issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance was established. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user systemprompt/appendsystemprompt, permission modes/rules, profiles, project settings and generic AskUser questions. - Closure path: configuration is loaded into sessions and generic questions can return user input, but no function-specific S5 issue-and-return path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent/operator authority over configuration is real, but Methodology 0.3.5 does not treat editable policy text, permission gates or generic human input as S5 by themselves. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/configuration/preference.mdx, docs-site/docs/configuration/permission.mdx, protocol/AskUser documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a wider organization embedding Ante may provide S5, but that authority is outside this standalone harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1/assessments/ante.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85d7c52a6a7a091b79de156dc8d709c08773eff1\">85d7c52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T22:13:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Linear Coding Agent Harness</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/coleam00/Linear-Coding-Agent-Harness/tree/c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c37dc096993011cb0cc48a0afab176cd9c8f2375\">c37dc09 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver working application features from a supplied specification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, failed behavior, tool errors and incomplete features. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation actions, tests, fixes and verified feature completion. - Decision owner: initializer/coding model agents in the concrete shipped SDK composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude SDK tool loop, local coding tools, Linear MCP, Puppeteer, Bash allowlist and session driver. - Closure path: agent observes file/tool/browser results, fixes the implementation, commits work and records issue completion with evidence for the next session. - Boundary reachability: runautonomousagent loads the shipped prompts and createclient tool configuration directly; custom agent organization is unnecessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime continuation cannot implement a feature without agent choices; supplied requirements constrain outcomes rather than every action. - Evidence: agent.py, client.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, prompts/initializerprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: avoid duplicate/conflicting feature ownership among project-working sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two coding agents acting on the same feature and interrupted-session work being mistaken for free work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret In Progress ownership, avoid another actor&#39;s claimed work and publish a claim before implementation. - Decision owner: an autonomous coordinating/peer arrangement still needs composition when multiple instances operate; the shipped serial instance and status convention alone do not close authoritative concurrent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear issue statuses, comments and per-session checks; no atomic compare-and-swap claim is asserted. - Closure path: claim/status and handoff become visible to later peer reads, changing issue eligibility; trustworthy attribution and concurrent conflict resolution remain composition obligations. - Boundary reachability: coding prompt Steps 2, 5, 6 and workflow prohibitions explicitly expose the collision-specific convention through configured Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific feedback primitive exists, but multiple-instance authority/recovery is not fully wired; no S2=A claim. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing project feature sessions when the documented other-agent convention is used. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping work on an already claimed issue. - Attenuating coordination relation: publish In Progress and refrain from working another agent&#39;s In Progress issue. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: subsequent Linear status read directs the peer toward a different eligible issue or resumed own work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited rule explicitly regulates duplicate ownership rather than merely storing or routing tasks. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Serial default does not itself demonstrate concurrent regulation. The prompt also prioritizes interrupted In Progress work; distinguishing that from another active owner&#39;s claim is left to composition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments and project stability across the feature portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a previously reported completed feature regresses while new feature work competes for attention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reopen a completed feature, divert work to its repair before adding features and choose the next eligible priority from current project state. - Decision owner: current coding-session agent under the shipped project-wide role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Linear portfolio/status/META reads and writes, browser access and local tools; iteration cap merely bounds execution. - Closure path: directly observed regression causes Done→In Progress and repair; updated state/notes constrain subsequent feature selection and later sessions. - Boundary reachability: codingprompt Steps 2 and 4 run before Step 5; the concrete client supplies list/update/comment and raw app access in every normal continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent judges operational evidence and intervenes on project commitments; deterministic delay/cap does not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: all project Done/Todo/In Progress counts, META progress, app specification, Git and sampled implemented behavior. - Current-control decision scope: project feature priorities, recovery of broken commitments and authority to suspend new feature development for repair. - Evidence: prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge predecessor session completion claims through complementary direct application inspection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Linear Done/comments can remain positive while actual app behavior has regressed or was misreported. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose 1–2 previously Done core features, judge their current UI reality and invalidate a prior completion when contradicted. - Decision owner: fresh continuation coding agent, independent of the predecessor context being checked. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh SDK client, Linear claims, Puppeteer navigation/screenshots and issue update tools. - Closure path: a failed sampled check reopens the issue, records what broke and forces repair before new work; findings also enter META for later control. - Boundary reachability: the installed coding prompt mandates predecessor-feature checks before new implementation and client.py provides those browser/Linear tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh agent makes the sampled audit judgment; tool output and status machinery only provide access and persistence. - Claim being audited: previous-session Done status and implementation/verification comments. - Ordinary reporting path: Linear issue statuses, implementation comments and META session summaries. - Complementary access path: direct browser operation and screenshots of selected already-completed core app features. - Independence boundary: fresh client/context checks predecessor claims without the predecessor&#39;s private conversation; same model family and serial role do not prove stronger institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: the current agent reopens/repairs and future sessions consume the changed portfolio. - Evidence: agent.py, prompts/codingprompt.md, client.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Credited only for the pre-work sample of predecessor Done claims. The same agent&#39;s Step 8 QA of its own new feature is ordinary production testing and is not additional S3. Static mandated path, not an observed audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: only current application feature/defect requirements are regulated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: initializer planning and session pacing allocate existing application work, not prospective harness adaptation. - Decision owner: no function-specific first-party adaptation owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: app specification, META memory, feature priorities and browser feedback. - Closure path: no external/future distinctions→adaptation options→present harness capability return found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: project planning and bug recovery do not themselves close S4. - Evidence: prompts/initializerprompt.md, prompts/codingprompt.md, agent.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational task safety and supplied application scope, without a policy-dispute loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: static role, app spec and command permissions constrain execution; no ultimate-policy adjudication path is shipped. - Decision owner: user/developer sets initial requirements and configuration; no runtime S5 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK permissions, sandbox configuration, allowlist hook and fixed prompt rules. - Closure path: no identity issue→legitimate authority→returned authoritative policy path found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: initial requirements and tool authorization are not runtime identity governance. - Evidence: client.py, security.py, prompts/codingprompt.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded source inspection. - Caveats: Static supported-path assessment; no live model/provider execution or effectiveness claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a/assessments/li/linear-coding-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3b92b0844ae8313e11e6ade32409a9d1d9eca54a\">3b92b08 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T22:55:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-openagent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/tree/879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 879a8b791e511edee68bada80187e0febb9b1aac\">879a8b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Team members are built-in autonomous agents that claim assigned or available work and execute it in their own worktrees. Their model/tool loops produce the operational outcomes of the team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Team Mode closes a concrete coordination loop among distinct S1s: atomic task claims prevent duplicate ownership, dependency gates suppress premature work, isolated member worktrees attenuate concurrent file interference, and the lead/member agents autonomously react to blockers and task availability. This is more than a mailbox or delegation graph because the mechanisms change later worker behavior in response to shared interference state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Team Lead has a whole-team view of tasks, messages and member status and is instructed to assign/reassign work, resolve blockers, manage member lifecycle, monitor progress and own project-level completion before synthesis. Those current-control decisions are made by a first-party autonomous lead agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Specialist/reviewer-shaped agents exist, but the reviewed Team Mode evidence does not establish a materially independent audit channel with distinct access and corrective closure over the operating team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Research/delegation specialists gather information for current work; no distinct outside-looking prospective organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. User prompts, agent definitions and team instructions provide operating goals and role constraints, not an ultimate organizational identity/policy tension-and-resolution loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd/assessments/oh-my-openagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd\">9b431df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T20:14:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tale</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tale-project/tale/tree/b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b2560c2d7d3294993e66129c891be4a059f3d6c4\">b2560c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce project task deliverables through agent-owned execution of assigned task briefs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous user requests, files and tool observations, provider responses, task feedback, source changes and execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: within an authorized turn, the configured autonomous agent chooses substantive task actions, tool interactions, corrections and the output to return. - Decision owner: configured model-driven project-agent actor in Tale&#39;s supported project-agent run; Tale does not claim first-party ownership of the selected external runtime&#39;s internal agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task assignment, task brief/context injection, sandbox session/worker, credential/tool bridge, output harvesting, run deadlines and agent retry. - Closure path: task brief and current project context → selected agent turn in its sandbox → model-driven actions/tool observations → updated actions and deliverables → persisted task report/artifacts and reviewer feedback → subsequent task work. - Boundary reachability: normal project-agent task starts create a standing sandbox turn for a configured supported harness; the implementation and task tools are shipped with Tale. No contributor-only executor is needed, though a configured external agent runtime/provider is a declared operational dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the configured agent actor leaves task/sandbox/queue infrastructure but no substantive choice of task actions or deliverable; the host does not decide content. - Evidence: run host launch and settle; tool bridge task actions; README supported runtimes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported composed agent-run path; no claim about autonomous management on a default unconfigured install. - Caveats: model providers and selectable external agent runtimes are dependencies. Their own S2-S5 are not inherited by Tale. A describes the reachable assembled S1 actor, not an autonomous Tale-owned model implementation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate operational interference between concurrent project-agent work cells through isolated workers, per-task single-live-run admission and capacity-aware waiting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two attempted runs on the same task, multiple task turns sharing a worker&#39;s files, and agents competing for sandbox host execution capacity. - Distinct S1 units: separate project-agent task runs, potentially simultaneous on distinct workers/tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping starts would collide on task ownership/review state; reused shared workspaces risk stale file contamination, while shared host capacity can cause execution contention. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party task-run admission/status guards and worker-scoped workspace/delivery box, capacity queues and optional host heavy-phase leases. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denied/already-running starts do not spawn another worker; queued starts wait for available capacity; a settled turn frees its worker slot, allowing later authorized work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the paths target explicit concurrent-run/cross-task file/host-contention disturbances, not merely task handoff or a mailbox. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide that conflicting starts should be refused or capacity-parked and that isolation/leases should govern subsequent starts. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic platform safety and admission rules; no Tale agent is established as owning a discretionary project-wide S2 coordination response in the credited base mode. This is a bounded S2-specific constructor/coordination path rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transactional run gates, standing worker allocation, task-specific output boxes, capacity retries and host lease tools. - Closure path: competing/oversubscribed start → guarded admission, isolated worker or capacity park → next start resumes only after resource/turn clearance. - Boundary reachability: ordinary shipped task-run/worker path; no extension-only coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing platform admission rules requires developer/operator composition; the configured work agent does not choose the S2 collision policy. C is credited to its concrete S2-specific path, not generic ability to send messages. - Evidence: standing worker isolation; task-specific delivery box; host lease admission; start tool occupancy semantics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: mechanical isolation and exclusive-run rules do not imply that a model agent owns coordination discretion; host leases alone are not an organizational S2 decision.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current project-wide task priorities, assigned operational commitments and intervention/review handling across multiple agent work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing unfinished tasks, changing assignments, blocked reviews, stale execution/review state and limited project-agent capacity. - Whole-system current view: project/board-wide taskfind lists task status, priority/assignee and captured review; taskget exposes task dependencies, current run and pending review. - Current-control decision scope: choose priority/agent assignment, start/resume another qualified agent, redirect a captured review to a different eligible independent agent, and decide which work receives next attention; operator task views/actions can exercise parent control separately. - Decisive decision or feedback right: project-wide assignment/priority and review-intervention choices based on current task portfolio, beyond one task&#39;s own model/tool steps. - Decision owner: base mode — explicitly granted manager agent with project-wide task authority; parent mode — legitimate project operator/editor acting through normal board/project task controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped tool grants, expected-value/CAS task mutations, current run identity, protected review transfer, capacity checks and status writes. - Closure path: manager&#39;s project task view → discretionary priority/assignment/review-transfer choice → guarded platform write → later agent starts/reviews use revised state; alternatively the human project operator chooses and commits an equivalent update through the UI/API. - Boundary reachability: Tale ships supported task-find/get/update/start/delegate-review tools for project agents; manager rights must be explicitly granted in the project. Direct operator task management is a first-party platform mode. Neither is a repository-maintainer or adjacent dogfood actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager mode, remove the manager&#39;s judgment and no project-wide choice of which task/agent/review to prioritize remains, only platform guards. In parent mode the operator owns that choice; neither platform lock nor scheduler is itself the decision owner. - Evidence: project-wide task observation; manager priority, assignment and review delegation; native task writes and actor paths; project task review and task UI. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for optional configured manager and project operator modes. - Caveats: having a manager label or an automatic scheduler alone is not evidence. The decisive right here is the explicitly scoped live manager&#39;s discretionary whole-project task-choice path. Ordinary operator approval of one output does not supply the S3 parent claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an agent-produced task completion claim using source-bound artifacts and a distinct reviewer agent, returning a corrective or approving decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misleading or stale self-reports, missing/inadequate output evidence, self-review conflicts, and acceptance claims unsupported by the actual task artifacts. - Claim being audited: the implementation agent&#39;s settled run and current task evidence support completion/acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation run&#39;s task comment and harvested deliverables enter inreview; the implementing agent cannot move its own task to done. - Complementary access path: a designated distinct review agent can read the captured source run, access available input/output files via guarded stagefile, perform independent checks and submit structured check/PR evidence. - Independence boundary: the reviewer must differ from implementationAgentId, must have the captured pending review/grant and matching evidence revision; human-competence and human-only gates cannot be bypassed. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agent itself chooses approve/requestchanges. A serializable review transaction atomically moves the task to done or back to todo, posts feedback and records the decision; a separate implementation agent can then repair the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judging the result of a separate operational run from independently accessed artifacts rather than accepting worker status. - Decision owner: authorized distinct reviewing model agent; platform validates source identity and commits the verdict but does not choose approve/requestchanges. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source/evidence-revision CAS, staged files, structured checks, independent reviewer-ID guard, native approval record and audit log. - Closure path: settled worker run → captured evidence and independent reviewer access → reviewer verdict + explanation → guarded task status/feedback transaction → acceptance or a subsequent correction run. - Boundary reachability: a native first-party project-agent review mode is shipped; the reviewer must be separately granted the task-review tool and assigned an eligible captured review. This is not Tale OSS CI and not merely the worker&#39;s own routine tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewing agent leaves a schema/permission gate but no independent verdict on artifacts. The worker cannot impersonate the independent reviewer; the model reviewer chooses the corrective judgment. - Evidence: captured source and distinct reviewer validation; native verdict and returned task status; review file staging and reviewer tool contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported independently assigned agent-review mode. - Caveats: the server cannot independently verify the substantive truth of every reviewer-supplied check, and native review is not necessarily a default path for every project. This positive finding relies on a separate authorized review agent examining actual evidence, not on the deterministic verdict parser or generic review label.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct external-and-prospective project capability adaptation loop is established beyond task work and current automation/operational controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tale agents can research outside information for their assigned tasks and automation can schedule later runs, but these do not alone generate and return a project-level future capability strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported first-party S4-level prospective adaptation authority and return into capability is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task research/web tools, project history, automation editors/schedules, project instruction editing, agent/model configuration, recurring tasks and task-level planning. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model carrying out a foresight research task is S1 unless its result enters an established project capability-adaptation conversation. A schedule or prompt change does not itself close that distinct loop. - Evidence: task tool catalog; platform automations; project instructions. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: operators could compose future planning and adaptation tasks, but a hypothetical configured workflow is not a shipped independent S4 closure. No claim is made that future implementations cannot supply one.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish durable project-purpose and governing instructions that subsequent project-agent operation must obey. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project goal drift, incompatible instructions, unauthorized scope changes, and changes to the owner&#39;s project-level operating rules. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for, what instructions/constraints its agents operate under, and which rules are authoritative across future task runs. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: a legitimate authorized project owner/editor changes project instructions; a task worker or manager is not shown to possess a shipped autonomous right to redefine this project identity without separately granted parent authority. - Return-to-operation path: updated project instructions are persisted in the project record and read into the agent-run task context for later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve and persist the project&#39;s authoritative durable instructions. - Decision owner: authorized parent project editor/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project permissions, expected hash and audit logging, durable project table, mandatory context-injection helpers, run instruction snapshot. - Closure path: project identity/instruction change proposed → authorized editor chooses content → version-checked persistent update → later task-agent launch reads the project instructions and the agent&#39;s work is governed by them. - Boundary reachability: first-party supported project configuration/update operation and standard run preparation. No contributor governance or unshipped policy agent is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent task tools can act within granted scope but do not by themselves select legitimate ultimate project-purpose changes; the parent editor holds the decision while the platform records and returns it. A task-level approval and static safety filter alone would not establish S5. - Evidence: authorized persistent project-instruction update; task-run instruction capture; project agent instructions. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this parent mode concerns project purpose/operating identity, not all platform-wide security policy or the Tale repository&#39;s maintainer decisions. No internal autonomous S5 decision path is credited.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a/assessments/ta/tale.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3d84f95617c8dfaa9fad883bc20aaad7cbd9922a\">3d84f95 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenDFM/Xcientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Xcientist</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenDFM/Xcientist/tree/666eee404bc3cf59249dc8af0ab621cc3e804a49\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 666eee404bc3cf59249dc8af0ab621cc3e804a49\">666eee4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing research work by constructing and executing an experiment project and iterating from observed execution/review feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing research proposals, incomplete project state, code/runtime failures, scientific-condition outcomes, artifacts and reviewer findings requiring context-sensitive work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete code/project actions and repairs needed to satisfy a work-unit/phase contract and produce executable scientific artifacts. - Decision owner: the active first-party Experiment Agent worker/planner agent sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase runner, workspace/manifests, deterministic hooks, execution tools, phase contracts, artifact ledger and fixed master sequencing. - Closure path: proposal/work-unit context -&gt; agent chooses project/code action -&gt; runtime executes or writes against the workspace -&gt; execution/review evidence returns -&gt; agent revises or completes the work unit. - Boundary reachability: the Experiment Agent and its vendored harness are a documented shipped Xcientist CLI/pipeline surface, not a development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic hooks and the master can block or transport state, but the context-sensitive scientific implementation/repair choice remains agent-owned. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/runtime/phaserunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple stacks/workers exist, but no specific inter-S1 interference, conflict or oscillation is identified and attenuated by a first-party coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific discretionary coordination decision is established. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential pipeline handoffs, planner-to-worker delegation, phase ordering, manifests and shared workspace artifacts. - Closure path: these surfaces sequence/delegate work but do not close an interference -&gt; coordination response -&gt; changed subsequent S1 behaviour loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker/reviewer plurality and pipeline topology are not themselves evidence of S2. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned whole-system current-control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: phase readiness, incomplete evidence and failed checks are regulated, but through fixed rules/gates rather than discretionary whole-system current control over shared commitments/resources/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the master computes RUNCODE, RUNSCIENCE or CONVERGED from fixed phase status conditions; no autonomous actor is shown revising organization-wide current commitments or priorities from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic MasterAgent, phase reports, readiness flags, blocking issues and fixed code-then-science ordering. - Closure path: deterministic phase state -&gt; fixed gate decision -&gt; run/stop/advance; this is lifecycle enforcement, not a discretionary S3 control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the master leaves no phase enforcement, but the organizational choice is encoded in fixed logic rather than owned by an autonomous agent. - Evidence: src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary Experiment Agent work claims before phase completion and return blocking findings into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: semantically wrong but superficially executable code, idea/protocol mismatch, scientific-invariant violations, weak integration evidence, reproducibility gaps and stale/unclean implementation paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue an independent PASS/FAIL judgment with blocking issues and required fixes after reading work artifacts and deterministic-hook evidence. - Decision owner: separate first-party read-only reviewer agents selected by reviewer role (ideaalignment, implementationcorrectness, scientificinvariants, protocolsemantics, integrationsmoke, reproducibility, codecleanliness, with analogous science review paths). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic hooks, review schemas, checked-artifact capture, immutable attempt reports, prefinish contracts and phase gate enforcement. - Closure path: worker claims completion -&gt; deterministic evidence is materialized -&gt; separate read-only reviewer inspects independent artifact/context surface -&gt; FAIL findings include requiredfix -&gt; same worker session receives the fix request and revises -&gt; reviewer/gate is rerun before completion. - Boundary reachability: reviewers and prefinish hooks are built into the shipped Experiment Agent phase runner and are required for phase completion, not benchmark-only code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hooks enforce invariant checks, but the non-formal semantic review judgment is made by a distinct reviewer agent; the phase runner transports that judgment back to the worker. - Claim being audited: that the current experiment work unit correctly implements the idea/protocol and is sufficiently integrated/reproducible to advance. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report plus normal execution/hook outputs. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives read-only access to the idea, shared review context, hook findings, worker report and relevant project artifacts and is instructed to inspect them independently. - Independence boundary: reviewer is a separate read-only agent invocation with a dedicated review role and cannot repair files; the audited worker owns implementation. - Who acts on findings: the Experiment Agent worker session, under phase-runner enforcement. - Evidence: src/agents/experimentagent/agents/code/reviewer.py, src/agents/experimentagent/runtime/phaserunner.py, src/agents/experimentagent/README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external/future-relevant scientific distinctions, develop a research option, and return the selected option into present experimentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external literature, related-work structure, novelty/feasibility distinctions and alternative research hypotheses that should alter what the experiment organization attempts next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: synthesize surveyed literature/retrieval/memory into a structured proposal and choose/refine prospective research ideas through the Idea Agent&#39;s model-driven process. - Decision owner: first-party Survey/Idea agent stack, especially the Idea Agent (LigAgent) operating over survey-grounded retrieval and Memory-Guided MCTS. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper retrieval/clustering, graph-backed references, shared memory, structured idearesult.json handoff and pipeline runner. - Closure path: external literature/topic -&gt; Survey synthesis/retrieval state -&gt; Idea Agent generates/refines a prospective proposal -&gt; idearesult.json -&gt; Experiment Agent consumes the proposal and changes present scientific work. - Boundary reachability: Survey, Idea and the pipeline handoff are documented first-party CLI/runtime surfaces and are directly connected to Experiment in the shipped prototype pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrieval and pipeline serialization support the loop, but the prospective research-option synthesis/refinement is agent-owned. - External distinction: papers, topic clusters, graph-backed related work and other retrieved scientific context. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a candidate research direction is sufficiently differentiated/interesting and how it should be formulated for future experiment work. - Adaptation option generated: a structured research proposal/idea with experimental direction. - Path back into current capability / S3: pipeline handoff from Survey -&gt; Idea -&gt; Experiment causes the current Experiment Agent to build and run a project for the selected proposal. - Evidence: README.md, src/cli.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the Xcientist organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: topic, model/provider, experiment configuration and reviewer contracts constrain a run, but they do not constitute an identity-level conflict-resolution loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration files, research topic/idea inputs, phase contracts, reviewer policies and CLI parameters. - Closure path: configuration constrains execution directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; return-to-operation loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no agent is shown owning the final authority to redefine or reconcile Xcientist&#39;s organizational identity/ultimate policy during operation. - Evidence: README.md, src/agents/experimentagent/agents/master/entry.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/xcientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T22:00:21Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/framerslab/agentos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentOS</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/framerslab/agentos/tree/fa3ec4a47f8521026e8c10aa876d082152b289d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fa3ec4a47f8521026e8c10aa876d082152b289d8\">fa3ec4a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user/operator objectives through model-driven agent loops that select tools/delegations, consume returned observations and continue toward an operational result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request ambiguity, intermediate model/tool results, external API/file/tool evidence, failures, and capability/role differences that change the next useful action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive model responses, tool calls, delegation targets/subtasks and follow-up actions within configured bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven AgentOS agent; in hierarchical agency mode the manager model owns the substantive delegation sequence while delegated agents autonomously execute their assigned local work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool execution, bounded step counts, guardrails, schemas, retries, permissions, deterministic strategy plumbing and optional HITL constraints. - Closure path: request enters AgentOS → model selects response/tool/delegation → first-party runtime executes the selected path → observations/sub-agent outputs return into model context → model chooses subsequent action or completes → result returns to the user/environment. - Boundary reachability: ordinary agent/agency operation and hierarchical model-driven delegation are first-party documented runtime paths at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic compilers and execution machinery transport/enforce decisions, but removing the model actor removes the substantive selection of tools, delegated work and response progression; the same decisions are not reproduced by static runtime rules. - Evidence: README.md; src/api/runtime/strategies/hierarchical.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: deterministic sequential/graph paths and lightweight helper modes also exist. A is based on reachable model-owned operating paths, not on every strategy transition being autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate substantive disagreement among distinct agent S1 contributions by making peer positions available for mutual adjustment and converging the resulting conflict into a coherent agency response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different operational agents can produce incompatible positions, omit one another&#39;s evidence or continue talking past each other, leaving the agency with unresolved contradictory output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each debate agent decides how to change its next argument in response to peer claims, and the final synthesizer decides how the competing positions are reconciled into one verdict/output. - Decision owner: distributed model-driven debate agents plus the model-driven synthesis agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic round sequencing, accumulated transcript construction, per-agent invocation, round caps and final synthesis prompt. - Closure path: multiple S1 agents produce positions → accumulated peer arguments are injected into later agent turns → agents autonomously rebut/refine in response → changed arguments enter subsequent rounds → synthesizer autonomously resolves the collected conflict into one agency result. - Boundary reachability: debate is a shipped first-party agency() strategy; callers select it directly without supplying their own coordination transport or reconciliation implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime determines when transcript material is passed, but it does not predetermine how an S1 responds to another S1&#39;s claim or which arguments ultimately prevail; those coordination judgments are model-owned. - Evidence: src/api/runtime/strategies/debate.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic graph dependencies, sequential routing, shared memory and message buses are not credited as S2. The positive mapping is specifically the debate-mode conflict/mutual-adjustment relation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more named agency agents with independent model calls/instructions that each produce an operational contribution to the same agency outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: materially conflicting or incomplete positions from different agents can leave the agency&#39;s response incoherent or blind to objections; the debate strategy deliberately surfaces this disagreement rather than assuming independent outputs compose safely. - Attenuating coordination relation: every non-opening debate turn receives the accumulated arguments from prior peer units and is instructed to attack the strongest opposing claim and add a new point; a final model-driven synthesis adjudicates the resulting positions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: peer arguments become input to later S1 model calls, directly changing what those agents consider and how they respond before final synthesis. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the transcript and round scheduler are only supporting channels; credit comes from their explicit use to expose and regulate inter-S1 disagreement through mutual rebuttal and reconciliation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current whole-runtime commitments and aggregate resource exposure through active-workflow admission and agency-level resource limits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many simultaneously active workflows or aggregate agency consumption can exceed configured current capacity/cost/time/call bounds and threaten the ability of the runtime to honor its existing commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise whole-runtime concurrency and aggregate resource limits, together with the response policy when those limits are reached. - Decision owner: no autonomous first-party S3 actor owns those choices. The downstream host/operator supplies the control regime; first-party runtime components measure and enforce it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkflowEngine.activeWorkflowCount, maxConcurrentWorkflows, workflow store/progress APIs, agency aggregate usage/call/duration accounting, checkLimits() and configured error/warn behavior. - Closure path: current workflow/agency load is observed → configured whole-system threshold is evaluated → excess new workflow commitment is rejected or a configured agency-limit response is raised → subsequent admitted work is constrained by that regime. - Boundary reachability: WorkflowEngine and agency controls are shipped first-party runtime surfaces and are directly configurable by an adopter without replacing the underlying control machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime has real whole-system state and enforcement authority, but the organizational choice of concurrency/resource budget and limit-response policy is not made by an autonomous model actor. The function-specific decision path is therefore left for downstream construction, yielding C. - Evidence: src/orchestration/workflows/WorkflowEngine.ts; src/api/agency.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: the hierarchical manager is not credited as S3 merely because it delegates work. Its documented scope is one-request task accomplishment. Likewise static limits do not become autonomous simply because the runtime enforces them. - Whole-system current view: active workflow count and workflow/progress state at the full WorkflowEngine level, plus aggregate agency usage, elapsed duration and agent-call counts for the agency-level control surface. - Current-control decision scope: admission of additional concurrent workflow commitments and aggregate agency resource/limit regime; the decisive policy values remain host-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit path is established for the declared AgentOS operating boundary at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review-loop, standalone Evaluator/LLMJudge utilities, emergent capability judges, tests, tracing and provenance were inspected but do not close S3 for ordinary operations. - Closure path: no complementary operating-audit closure established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the review-loop reviewer consumes the producer&#39;s ordinary draft plus task and is part of the normal production QA path; standalone evaluator surfaces are not wired into current operational control; emergent judges audit proposed capabilities inside S4 adaptation rather than independently sampling ordinary S1/S3 reality. - Evidence: src/api/runtime/strategies/review-loop.ts; src/safety/evaluation/Evaluator.ts; docs/architecture/EMERGENTCAPABILITIES.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: detect externally presented capability gaps, develop and test new executable capability options, and return approved adaptations into the agent&#39;s future operating repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tasks and environmental demands can require a capability absent from the agent&#39;s currently registered tools, while candidate generated capabilities may be unsafe, incorrect or unreliable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model agent decides that a capability gap warrants forging and specifies a new capability; model-driven judging participates in deciding whether the candidate is safe/correct enough to enter the repertoire, within deterministic safety/test bounds. - Decision owner: the model-driven operating agent owns the adaptation proposal; the first-party LLM judge owns the semantic approval judgment for creation/promotion subject to hard runtime constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forgetool, composable and sandbox builders, declared test cases, schema validation, hardened node:vm, EmergentJudge, EmergentToolRegistry, session/agent/shared tiers and deterministic usage/confidence thresholds. - Closure path: an external task exposes a missing capability → model invokes forgetool and proposes/composes implementation → first-party tests and LLM judge evaluate the option → approved capability is registered at session tier → subsequent turns can invoke it by name → sufficiently proven capabilities can move to a longer-lived agent tier, changing future operating capability. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents AgentOS.create({ emergent: true }) as a full-runtime first-party entry point that initializes emergent support and exposes forgetool; the positive claim does not rely on the lightweight agent() helper, which explicitly does not activate the path on its own. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic sandbox, schemas and thresholds constrain the search space, but they do not decide which missing capability to invent or semantically judge the candidate. Removing the model proposal/judge actors removes those adaptation decisions. - Evidence: docs/architecture/EMERGENTCAPABILITIES.md; src/cognition/emergent/ForgeToolMetaTool.ts; src/cognition/emergent/EmergentCapabilityEngine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: emergent capability creation is opt-in rather than default. Opt-in does not negate boundary reachability because the full runtime ships the complete decision/feedback path. Generic memory/self-improvement alone is not used as the S4 witness. - External distinction: a task/environment presents a required operation for which the current capability catalog has no suitable tool. - Future / prospective distinction: the runtime does not merely improvise one answer; it creates a named executable capability intended for subsequent turns, tracks reliability and provides a path to longer-lived agent-tier reuse. - Adaptation option generated: a composed or sandboxed typed tool implementation with schemas and tests, proposed by the model and semantically reviewed before activation. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved tools enter the first-party EmergentToolRegistry and become callable by name on later turns; promotion can persist a proven capability at agent tier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and preserve agent identity, values, tone and hard behavioral limits through a durable parent-authored SOUL workspace that governs subsequent runtime behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/context drift or operational adaptation could otherwise make the agent&#39;s enduring identity, values and ultimate behavioral boundaries ambiguous across sessions and changing tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define or revise the identity-level content of SOUL.md, including values and hard limits that bound subsequent operation. - Decision owner: the legitimate parent/operator who owns the agent workspace; no autonomous first-party actor is given ultimate authority to rewrite the SOUL identity/hard-limit source. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL workspace convention, YAML-to-IPersonaDefinition parsing, SoulLoader, system-message injection, structured persona fields and runtime prompt/persona machinery. - Closure path: identity/policy content is authored or revised by the parent in SOUL.md → first-party loader reads it at agent boot → structured identity/hard limits and prose enter runtime persona/system context → subsequent model operation is governed by that parent decision. - Boundary reachability: SOUL workspaces and souledAgent()/loadSoul are documented first-party runtime paths, with SOUL.md explicitly required for the souled identity mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AgentOS can autonomously edit its memory wiki and can optionally adapt bounded personality parameters, but those lower-level adaptation paths do not transfer ultimate authority over the durable SOUL identity, values and hard limits away from the parent. - Evidence: docs/SOULFILES.md; src/cognition/substrate/personas/SoulLoader.ts; src/cognition/substrate/personas/SOUL.template.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: a generic system prompt alone would not establish S5. Credit is based on the repository&#39;s identity-specific SOUL boundary, explicit values/hard-limit semantics and closed load-back path. Bounded runtime personality adaptation is not treated as autonomous ultimate-policy authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who the agent is, what values it embodies and which hard behavioral limits remain authoritative across changing tasks/sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed mode only — the workspace owner/operator authors the identity source; no autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: SOUL.md is loaded at boot into structured persona fields and the leading system context, which directly governs later agent decisions and responses.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/db7e6211a5dcc311babd508512a358210aeb3c36/assessments/agentos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit db7e6211a5dcc311babd508512a358210aeb3c36\">db7e621 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T19:05:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/romiluz13/cc10x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cc10x</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/romiluz13/cc10x/tree/36c7b06a7c846d18cfbf33969ee0a7a2483985af\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 36c7b06a7c846d18cfbf33969ee0a7a2483985af\">36c7b06 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement, debug, plan, review or execute a targeted coding/QA task through a specialized Claude model agent with tool-feedback iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code behavior, test failures, integration surprises and findings discovered during the assigned phase. - Decisive decision or feedback right: worker model selects investigative/implementation steps, reads tool outputs, edits code where authorized and returns contract-structured results. - Decision owner: dispatched Claude model agent such as component-builder, bug-investigator or QA-executor, according to task type. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompts, agent-common and domain skills, Claude Agent tool, workflow phase artifact, handoff contract checks. - Closure path: router&#39;s phase scope → model agent tools/result interpretation → implementation/evidence → structured report → router records outcome or returns remediation. - Boundary reachability: official plugin install documents main router + built-in agents, and default task graph calls Claude Agent primitive; separate Task tools are optional with supported artifact-only mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absent the specialized model, prompts and Python guards cannot choose substantive source edits or judge task evidence. - Evidence: router, builder workflow, README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for documented/installed role loop; live Claude coding run not reproduced. - Caveats: external Claude Code provider/Agent primitive required for isolated cells; inline fallback does not prove independent role execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party regulation of interaction-driven interference between distinct active S1 cells identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reviewer and failure-hunter may independently find issues, but they are read-only and their result aggregation is not an observed inter-agent resource collision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none specific to countering S1 peer interference. - Decision owner: no S2 owner established; task phase graph and conflicts in evidence are resolved by router current-control S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: blockedBy task dependencies, serial phase dispatch, parallel read-only review/hunt, artifact scope by workflow UUID and router result aggregation. - Closure path: no demonstrated peer interference → local coordination correction → changed affected S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel agents, role delegation, task dependency edges and validation sequencing are not enough without a concrete S2 variety witness. - Evidence: router, BUILD graph. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: router can handle conflicting audit opinions as current workflow control, but no distinct operational peer-contention loop has been proven.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control the entire current coding workflow across specialized worker, audit, verification, remediation and memory steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: contradictory evidence, incomplete phases, failed tests, growing scope, stale state, blocked permissions and budgeted remediation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: router model interprets user intent, selects workflow/complexity, authors dependent role graph, validates reports, decides remediation escalation versus pause, and re-enters phases until verified. - Decision owner: first-party instructed main-session Claude router agent, not state-persistence Python hooks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cc10x-router skill, UUID-scoped artifact/events, contract envelope validation, phaseexitgate, task tool or artifact-only graph, remediation history and stop rules. - Closure path: whole workflow state/agent findings → model router decides next phase or remediation → dispatch and persisted results → updated state and verifier feedback → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: plugin entry router is installed/activated for ordinary code requests and maintains authority in supported Agent dispatch mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic guards cannot choose the substantive phase topology, scope negotiation or remedy; those rights belong to router model. - Whole-system current view: router reconstructs all active phase state, task blockers, results and artifact history within the current workflow UUID and validates graph completion. - Current-control decision scope: role assignments, phase gates, escalation from trivial/full graph, remediation/re-review chain, accepted user clarifications and stop/continue. - Evidence: router, BUILD reference, state persistence. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model ownership and standard instructions. - Caveats: enforced prompt process depends on compliance by external Claude model, and human approval/permission input is not a separate S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation and planning success claims with fresh or read-only model evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: review anchoring, hallucinated findings, green tests that prove little, unverified user flows and premature phase completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent read-only integration-verifier executes tests, independently checks code/line evidence and returns PASS/FAIL to router; PLAN uses a fresh anti-anchored plan-gap-reviewer. - Decision owner: separately dispatched Claude model agents under verifier/reviewer prompts; router receives verdict and controls remediation, rather than self-auditing builder claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated Agent dispatch, verifier read-only tools, per-scenario evidence contract, test honesty sweeps, fresh reviewer forbidden to load planner memory, phase exit gate and remedial re-review. - Closure path: builder/plan output → independently grounded reviewer/verifier evidence → blocking verdict → router creates REM-FIX/re-review and re-verification; only checked PASS advances. - Boundary reachability: full BUILD, trivial BUILD verifier, DEBUG and PLAN/QA graphs ship mandatory review/verification roles in supported subagent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: another model agent explicitly reopens code, re-runs tests and adjudicates findings; deterministic gate and router relay rather than make the independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: builder task goal/implementation correctness, source findings and phase exit readiness; PLAN mode challenges execution-plan completeness. - Ordinary reporting path: builder/reviewer structured handoffs and workflow artifact state into the router. - Complementary access path: separate read-only verifier&#39;s own direct code/test/tool observations and fresh plan reviewer&#39;s original request/plan/source investigation. - Independence boundary: verifier has distinct Agent session/tool access and must treat prior claims as UNVERIFIED; fresh plan reviewer excludes prior planner rationale and memory. - Who acts on findings: router blocks phase advance and schedules bounded remediation or pauses for user, then invokes new verifier. - Evidence: verifier, fresh plan reviewer, router. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for standard independent model review-and-return path; live provider audit accuracy not claimed. - Caveats: supported inline fallback loses independent subagent session; no S3 A inference from inline mode alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate outward-facing prospective organizational adaptation cycle established beyond current workflow memory/replay. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failure and compaction are managed in present workflow; future external capability shifts not monitored in an evidenced closed path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party strategic future-option decision returned into present operating capability. - Decision owner: none for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reusable memory patterns, failure history, research agent, skills and session persistence. - Closure path: no established external horizon → future option → adopted change to current system method. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: saving lessons and researching inside a task improves context but does not constitute a distinct outside-and-then intelligence loop. - Evidence: router, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can request roadmap research as ordinary workflow work without creating S4 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/policy decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission gates, scope choices, review confidence and workflow opt-out are per-task operating constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity-level authority or subsequent governance return. - Decision owner: none for S5; Claude user permissions and project standards govern individual tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AskUserQuestion, user plan approval, permission settings, safety instructions and no-destructive-action rules. - Closure path: no identity-level proposal → legitimate authority → ultimate-policy return to agent operating mode. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approvals for edits, branch finishing, risk and plans do not govern organizational identity/ultimate policy. - Evidence: router, BUILD workflow. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human managers of actual projects may supply independent policy, outside this packaged plugin&#39;s recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/94438b773ef572a514b6a8b6068ef1a160dbae11/assessments/cc/cc10x.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 94438b773ef572a514b6a8b6068ef1a160dbae11\">94438b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T22:49:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope-java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentScope Java 2.0</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope-java/tree/3d1b998d7c1c0aa6d9697a85b59c205a4c7c2a0f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3d1b998d7c1c0aa6d9697a85b59c205a4c7c2a0f\">3d1b998 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed reasoning and tool action for supplied tasks, including delegated independent subtasks in team mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous inputs, tool results/failures, context size, state recovery and diverse task subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent chooses reasoning, next tool or teammate task action on feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed ReAct/HarnessAgent and configured team worker/lead agent roles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Toolkit, model/permission middleware, AgentStateStore, workspace/sandbox, tool transport and invocation limits. - Closure path: prompt/state → model choice → tool call → tool result/event → subsequent model decision or task completion. - Boundary reachability: ReActAgent is the packaged core and HarnessAgent.Builder creates it with installed middleware and model/tool registries. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model makes semantic operational selections, while Java classes enforce schema/permission and handle state/transport. - Evidence: ReActAgent.java, HarnessAgent.java, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for documented installed call path. - Caveats: hosted models, tools, data and application goals are caller-provided.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: mitigate competing work claims and contradictory or dependent teammate progress within one active team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple operational members may claim the same task, act before predecessors finish or report incompatible conclusions. - Distinct S1 units: multiple team-worker/lead model-backed HarnessAgent instances identified by team+member and operating on independent tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing task ownership and blockedBy dependencies; team lead prompt also requires resolution of disagreements among teammate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: a shared versioned task board implements exclusive compare-and-swap claims/assignments and dependency blocking; model lead sees team reports and can resolve disagreements/reassign work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflicts return as tool errors; successful assignment or completion wakes the affected member and injects the task/result into its next reasoning turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: unlike a generic mailbox or task fan-out, task-ownership CAS and dependency checks prevent structurally specified competing operations and the team lead is explicitly prompted to reconcile conflicting teammate conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task allocation/reassignment or reconcile disagreements; prevent double claims and notify the next legitimate worker. - Decision owner: lead/worker model agents for allocation and response to feedback; LocalTeamClient deterministically rejects invalid claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamClient, CAS store, task owner/blockedBy fields, TeamTool, TeamsMiddleware wakeups/inbox. - Closure path: attempted claim/assignment → conflict/dependency check → changed board or denial → model/worker wakes, selects eligible work or lead reallocates it. - Boundary reachability: HarnessAgent.Builder registers TeamsMiddleware and TeamTool when teamsModeClient/context are supplied; TeamsMiddleware feeds board and reconciliation instructions to live model reasoning, not only tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantics of allocation/reconciliation belong to team agent choices; CAS is a necessary integrity enforcer but not the owner of team task discretion. - Evidence: LocalTeamClient.java, TeamTask.java, TeamsMiddleware.java, TeamTool.java. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, conditional on team mode and genuine multiple members. - Caveats: the CAS subpath is deterministic; A relies on the wired model lead/worker feedback decisions in actual team operation, not CAS in isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control present team-wide commitments, task ownership and completion across multiple active workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a lead losing track of allocated work, failed tasks, idle team members and incomplete team commitments. - Whole-system current view: TeamsMiddleware.renderBoardDigest supplies the team lead with every task, state, owner, settled result, and the set of tasks it still owns; refreshed at each turn. - Current-control decision scope: lead model may create/assign/reassign tasks, spawn/shutdown members, reject/approve plans and complete a team only when the board is settled; worker actions are role-clipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model lead interprets board and member feedback, decides which current work to assign, rescue or close. - Decision owner: lead model-backed agent in configured team mode, not the deterministic board or external user. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-scoped TeamTool, board persistence, wakeups, CAS and terminal-state checks. - Closure path: whole-board digest/member failure notice → lead decides intervention → TeamTool updates assignment/team/membership → changed worker tasks and later digest. - Boundary reachability: source builder wires team middleware and tools into the actual model call, and live onReasoning injects refreshed whole-team board into the lead&#39;s system message. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model is instructed and equipped to reason over entire board and select interventions; runtime enforces ownership and delivery but has no equivalent semantic resource/commitment judgment without the lead. - Evidence: TeamsMiddleware.java (renderBoardDigest, renderTeamSection), TeamTool.java, HarnessAgent.java (team mode wiring). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for wired conditional mode. - Caveats: team governance is scoped to one run/team, not all externally deployed AgentScope users or cloud admin service.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate sufficiently independent inspection of ordinary team reports is established by the inspected runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: team worker errors, tool violations and misleading reports can be observed but only via ordinary logs, tests, task results and permission checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model lead receives worker reports through the same team task board; no separate independent operational-reality access/judgment/return exists in the standard team runtime. - Decision owner: regular team lead and configured verification hooks; not an independent audit unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTraceMiddleware, permission system, tests, task state and logs. - Closure path: results and tool denials reach ordinary operating agents; no independent audit findings flow to team-wide corrective authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reviewer subagent can be configured, but the generic declaration and agent trace alone cannot establish an independent complementary access path. - Evidence: TeamsMiddleware.java, AgentTraceMiddleware.java, subagent.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: independently built application-specific auditors remain possible outside the credited mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective option-development loop linked to adaptation of present team capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user requests and accumulated skill usage change task execution and skill hygiene, not a modeled future environment or strategic response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: optional agent may propose/edit skills; optional human/local gate promotes them; periodic curator archives stale skills and optionally dry-runs merges. - Decision owner: agent for task-local skill drafting, operator for promotion, deterministic curator schedule for hygiene; none demonstrably owns S4 prospective adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SkillManageTool, promotion filters/gates, SkillCuratorMiddleware, memory maintenance and usage counters. - Closure path: approved skills can affect later agent capabilities, but the required outside/future distinction and prospective strategic alternatives/feedback are not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-learning and durable skill changes are not sufficient for outside-and-then S4; human approval is not automatically S4 parent authority. - Evidence: skill.md, SkillCuratorMiddleware.java. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: caller-specific future intelligence built via middleware is outside the library&#39;s default function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed ultimate-policy/identity deliberation-and-return for the assessed agent/team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, team role scope, plan-mode approval and workspace policies limit current work but do not arbitrate mission or ultimate values. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer/operator sets tool permissions and team roles; the model receives role clipping and task/plan verdicts, not authority over identity-level policy. - Decision owner: user/configuration and application administrators outside a first-party constitutional return path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission middleware, TeamTool roles, plan approval, skill promotion gates. - Closure path: tool consent or plan verdict affects task execution, but there is no first-party identity question → legitimate authority → policy ratification → subsequent operation circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent&#39;s role or prompt cannot itself prove ultimate-policy authority; human task approvals do not become S5 parent governance. - Evidence: TeamTool.java, permission-system.md, skill.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a higher-level installed application may build governance; not credited to this standard Java harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e6ba041fd81345e79cfe7f292c3f45b4666a0be/assessments/ag/agentscope-java-2-0.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e6ba041fd81345e79cfe7f292c3f45b4666a0be\">4e6ba04 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:57:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mikeyobrien/ralph-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ralph Orchestrator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mikeyobrien/ralph-orchestrator/tree/b5789dfbb25bd4aa65ce565c756d95acc8c42f76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b5789dfbb25bd4aa65ce565c756d95acc8c42f76\">b5789df &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce working code, review or researched task artifacts through model-directed tool-use iterations against a real project environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source/tool outputs, failing tests, partial code, unavailable backend responses and unresolved task requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active backend agent selects investigation, edits, tests and event/continuation response, within the supplied role and user task. - Decision owner: model-backed agent session invoked in the supported Ralph loop, not the deterministic EventLoop dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: backend adapters, prompt/hats, event bus, tasks/memory/scratchpad, persisted iteration state and cost/runtime/failure guardrails. - Closure path: user objective → Ralph invokes model backend with current context → model executes tools and emits state/event → Ralph stores outcome and selects subsequent iteration/hat → subsequent model action or evidence-backed completion. - Boundary reachability: ralph run with a configured supported backend is the public CLI path, not a development-only demo; code-assist is a bundled preset. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model decision-making leaves an event dispatcher and durable state, not the task-specific source-changing choices. - Evidence: backend execution/iteration, event/hat dispatch, Builder&#39;s actual TDD/tool duty. - Basis: structural shipped execution path plus explicit role prescriptions and unit/E2E fixture coverage. - Confidence: high for available autonomous S1 path, without claiming a paid live model run in this review. - Caveats: Ralph depends on externally installed agent backends; hard runtime limits and fixed role prompts constrain but do not themselves own task action selection.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: reconcile conflicting outputs of distinct concurrently operating worktree-loop S1 cells before they can become one coherent repository state in the selected auto-merge mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently completed branches can modify overlapping files or introduce mutually incompatible behavior; unsynchronized merges can break the shared working tree. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing model-backed Ralph loops in distinct Git worktrees, each contributing a task branch rather than only successive hats of a single loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independent loop branches may edit the same file or cause a test failure when combined, so blindly admitting both would corrupt or destabilize the shared project. - Attenuating coordination relation: completed worktree loops submit to the durable merge queue; selected merge-loop mode uses a model-driven Branch Merger/Conflict Resolver and verification stages against the shared target branch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merge success makes the reconciled output available on main for later work; conflict/failing-test outputs lead the model to repair or leave an explicit needs-review state for the next operational decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path explicitly detects and resolves incompatible outputs of distinct operating loops at their common repository boundary, not merely delivering the next task event. - Decisive decision or feedback right: on a substantive conflict, the merge-loop model chooses how to preserve both branch intents and repair failing merged behavior; queue locks only transport and gate this discretion. - Decision owner: dedicated merge-loop model agent in the first-party preset; Git/file-lock state and test runner are supporting deterministic mechanisms. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree isolation, MergeQueue durable states, CLI subprocess spawning, Git merge/test invocations, conflict events and failure/needs-review escalation. - Closure path: independent loop outputs → queued branch → merge-loop conflict evidence/diff → model selects reconciliation/repair → changed main branch and verified outcome (or explicit unresolved state) → subsequent loops use new codebase. - Boundary reachability: parallel worktrees are supported; enabling shipped features.automerge actually enqueues and starts the bundled merge-loop preset after completion. The default manual-merge mode is not claimed to have autonomous S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the queue serializes transport but cannot semantically reconcile a C++/Rust/source conflict or choose correct combined behavior; a configured model agent does that in the positive mode. - Evidence: worktree completion queue, merge queue states and locks, merge-loop spawn, resolver/merger/tester roles, parallel and auto-merge defaults. - Basis: explicit configuration, positive source-level feedback path, and merge-queue/loop tests; no live merge conflict generated in this assessment. - Confidence: medium-high for selected supported coordination path; real conflict-resolution quality remains unmeasured. - Caveats: automerge is disabled by default and conflict resolution depends on the external model backend. In a solo or manual-merge-only deployment there is no positive S2 at the declared multi-loop relation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party model/parent controller with a whole-system current operating view and discretionary authority over all live loop commitments/resources was established for the assessed task/parallel-loop boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cost exhaustion, runtime failure, stale iterations, merge backlog and human-requested correction, mostly handled as local stop/retry/queue mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured budgets, maximum iterations, scheduler/role selection and operator interventions; these are preset/deployment policy or task-local operational choices, not an evidenced whole-system S3 judgment. - Decision owner: developer/operator for limits and selected task mode; backend agents for their own task work. No qualifying first-party S3 owner demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LoopState, TerminationReason, per-loop RObot status, merge queue, TUI/web status and runtime config. - Closure path: local failure/cost signals stop or retry a loop; there is no established decision from a total current operational view into revised cross-loop commitments or priorities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: stopping at a configured limit or displaying multiple loops does not transfer current-system resource discretion to an autonomous agent; merge reconciliation is S2, not whole-system S3. - Evidence: termination and state, configuration of limits and parallel mode, operator interface. - Basis: bounded structural negative after active role, loop, merge and operator path review. - Confidence: medium; whole-system control was not inferred from the product&#39;s “orchestrator” label. - Caveats: an external human project manager may provide S3 at a higher recursion, but their wider organization is not packaged as Ralph&#39;s first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementer&#39;s reported success with a fresh model-backed inspection of actual changed code, tests and failure paths, returning an independent correct/reject judgment to the implementation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive builder completion, hidden regression, failed requirement/edge case and claims based only on self-reported test success. - Claim being audited: Builder&#39;s review.ready assertion that its task-specific code increment and verification satisfy acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder emits review.ready after code/test work with task identity and claimed result. - Complementary access path: bundled code-assist Critic is a different hat/backend invocation, told to inspect actual changed source, rerun verification and an adversarial failure scenario instead of trusting Builder. The separate review-only preset provides an additional independently selectable direct-code inspection mode. - Independence boundary: role-separated Critic has independent review instructions, fresh turn context and an explicit pass/reject decision; no claim of external organizational independence or a separately audited provider model. - Who acts on findings: review.rejected activates Builder for repair; review.passed permits Finalizer to assess whole-task completion; subsequent task state reflects rejection/repair rather than accepting Builder&#39;s assertion automatically. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Critic model decides whether code evidence supports pass or requires rejection/correction. - Decision owner: Critic&#39;s separate model-backed role, not deterministic event routing or the Builder that authored the increment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review.ready event, hat subscriptions/publications, task IDs, required review event, backend execution and task/verification persistence. - Closure path: Builder source changes and review.ready → Critic independently inspects code/tests → review.rejected or review.passed → Builder repairs or Finalizer reviews completion → subsequent work acts on auditor judgment. - Boundary reachability: first-party published code-assist preset directly wires Builder/Critic/Finalizer and passes these events through ralph run, with no custom external audit plugin required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Critic&#39;s adverse code/test diagnosis is model discretion; the event bus only delivers its choice. Ordinary self-tests alone would not meet the complementary independence test. - Evidence: Critic, role separation, actual runtime/failure-path check and rejection, hat event routing, separate adversarial review preset. - Basis: explicitly shipped separate-agent review instructions and structurally closed pass/reject feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for selected mode; actual auditor independence/accuracy was not measured with live backend invocations. - Caveats: separate role invocation is narrower than external auditor independence; solo/preset-without-Critic runs do not acquire S3 from ordinary tests. This verdict is restricted to supported code-assist Critic mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped loop was established that senses externally/future-relevant changes, evaluates prospective options for Ralph&#39;s own operating capability and closes a strategic adjustment into current whole-system control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing coding-task details, research questions, locally measured improvement experiments and memory of task errors, rather than a sustained whole-system environmental-future strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent/Strategist may plan a project experiment or choose an implementation adjustment; operator chooses new goals/backends/presets. - Decision owner: task agent for bounded research/optimization operations; user/developer for Ralph&#39;s model/capability policy. No separate first-party S4 right established at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research and autoresearch task presets, memories, experiment logs, benchmark/rubric loops, configurable backend/hats. - Closure path: experimentation can improve the user&#39;s target artifact inside the active S1 task, but no evidenced future-environment model returns a capability/governance decision to a Ralph-level S3 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-driven experiment planning is not automatically S4; its object is usually an operational deliverable, with no separate S3–S4 long-horizon conversation. - Evidence: autoresearch experiment preset, research-only preset, core configuration. - Basis: structural negative with explicit consideration of shipped positive experiment modes. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a human can employ Ralph to study future technologies, but such use does not by itself install an S4 for the harness at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy decision channel with legitimate governing authority and return to the running harness was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator prompt changes, content/approval bounds, safe defaults, per-task human help, backend configuration and resource ceilings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/developer selects the task, configuration and permissions; the model can ask for task clarification but no identity-level matter is routed to a governing parent S5 by first-party protocol. - Decision owner: external user/deployment policy, not an autonomous Ralph S5 or evidenced operationally closed parent S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guards, configurations, RObot human.interact, Telegram commands, stop/restart, role prompts, Git safety/merge defaults. - Closure path: human guidance changes a task iteration or deployment config, but no identity/ultimate-policy proposal → legitimate parent ruling → returned durable policy decision is defined for this system-in-focus. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approvals and human messages regulate immediate tasks; static instructions are not themselves identity policy deliberation or S5 closure. - Evidence: human interaction/Telegram scope, loop event/termination policy, configurable support modes. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the maintainers&#39; development governance or a deployment owner&#39;s wider organization may supply higher-recursion S5, but that is not the selected packaged task/loop boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/88b0c10833fb3161c4ab5c521bfb7f878088ab1a/assessments/ra/ralph-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 88b0c10833fb3161c4ab5c521bfb7f878088ab1a\">88b0c10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T00:19:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nodecode-ai/nodecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nodecode</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nodecode-ai/nodecode/tree/314476ddee650954f32695500be308276243f28b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 314476ddee650954f32695500be308276243f28b\">314476d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed task transformation through Lisp, shell and installed capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task/environment changes, tool failure, context limits and background work completing later. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next eval/action or answer based on accumulated observations. - Decision owner: The configured task agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent-loop, provider adapters, durable session facts, eval dispatch, background registry, context/recall and mutation persistence. - Closure path: execute-tool-calls appends actual results; agent-loop recurs with updated context, reading background exits before final answers when necessary. - Boundary reachability: Public installed runtime supplies the agent-loop and eval tool directly; model APIs provide inference rather than an imported agent harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model actor there is persistence/enforcement but no equivalent task-dependent action selection. - Evidence: src/engine/turn.lisp; src/engine/exec.lisp; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for shipped ownership. - Caveats: No live provider, task-quality or sandbox test was run. The repository explicitly states that eval is not a security sandbox.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference and destructive convergence among parallel task agents sharing one directory/image/artifact. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate approaches, concurrent artifact replacement, peer function redefinition and oscillating adoption of unverified results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/revise a distinct approach, decide evidence-based adoption while preserving variation, and decide whether a candidate warrants promotion after rereading the shared best. - Decision owner: Team nodes mutually adjusting their work through the first-party agent protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic slot claims, private work-K directories, LOG/DISCONFIRMED/ADOPTED, verified scores, best.lock and kernel fork-name fencing. - Closure path: Nodes reread peers&#39; approaches/results, change a conflicting or dominated strategy, reproduce candidate evidence and conditionally replace best under the lock; later nodes observe the new state. - Boundary reachability: Installed pinned team cell constructs and submits these prompts to each minted full-tool session via start-nodes/node-prompt. No developer-supplied coordination agent is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks enforce ordering, while model nodes choose the substantive approach and adoption response. Removing their judgment leaves serialization without mutual strategy adjustment. - Distinct S1 units: Separately minted team-node sessions with task execution and local work areas. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared approach/artifact/image collisions and repeatedly replacing a peer&#39;s stronger result without reproduction. - Attenuating coordination relation: Explicit approach differentiation, periodic peer-evidence rereads, validated adoption with retained variation, and conditional locked promotion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Changed strategies and shared best/evidence influence subsequent node actions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped protocol explicitly names cross-node collisions/convergence and prescribes agent-chosen corrective adjustment, not merely message transport. - Evidence: team/cell.lisp prompt/start-nodes; team/README.md; src/organism/fork.lisp fork-fence. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the optional team mode. - Caveats: Board messaging or fork plurality alone would not establish S2. Coordination is behavioral rather than a security isolation guarantee.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Current regulation of the standing team&#39;s commitments and continuing resource use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Several active/idle/spent workers continuing an obsolete commitment or consuming budget under stale instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Redirect the standing team&#39;s work, stop the team or submit a new current commitment using its current collective state. - Decision owner: Legitimate operator of the local session/standing team. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-node usage folding, team roster/status, budget-hook, /team control, put-input steering and replacement of spent seats. - Closure path: /team reads current team state; /team TEXT reaches current nodes as a steer or follow-up and seats fresh nodes where spent nodes cannot continue; /team stop cancels the team. - Boundary reachability: These are installed native slash commands on the operator&#39;s session, with node sessions explicitly refused the right to seat another standing team. This is collective current control, not a generic single-action approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Node agents own local work; the operator supplies standing-team current commitments and intervention. Automatic budget enforcement/reseating does not select the operator&#39;s decision. - Whole-system current view: The standing-team roster, node activity/remaining spend and shared findings/artifact at the assessed team mode, exposed by result/result-text and /team. - Current-control decision scope: Team-wide work redirection, continuation/reseating and collective stop. - Evidence: team/cell.lisp node-spend/node-left, seat, put-input, run-slash, result/result-text; team/README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, explicitly limited to the standing-team mode. - Caveats: No autonomous S3 manager is claimed. An uninstantiated generic eval capable of management is not credited. Operator intervention over one ordinary tool call would be insufficient.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Post-turn challenge to operational claims and user-visible changes through separate verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A successful-looking task answer or write receipt despite an incorrect/unverified resulting state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reconstruct the intended invariant, choose narrow direct checks and adjudicate pass/fail/blocked, then repair within bounds or return actionable findings. - Decision owner: A separately instantiated experience reflection agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frame observer, frozen parent-turn anchor, dedicated child session, verification sightings, safe-tool fence, bounded repair budget and durable recap/attention delivery. - Closure path: Reflector reads actual results/durable facts, records evidence and verdict; a narrow supported repair is reverified, otherwise a next action/blocked finding returns to the originating session or operator. - Boundary reachability: The pinned experience cell&#39;s installed observer and worker invoke reflect-turn/fork-reflection after eligible task turns, using the bundled verification prompt and available safe verbs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker schedules reflection and stores its findings, but the separate agent chooses evidence and judges the invariant. Ordinary producer completion and deterministic receipt checks do not make that judgment. - Claim being audited: That user-visible code/config/data/live-surface changes and operational claims actually satisfy their intended invariant. - Ordinary reporting path: Original task agent answer, transcript and tool/write receipts. - Complementary access path: Separate reflector verification using existing safe verbs to read back actual result state and durable facts; its prompt explicitly rejects a write receipt as sufficient proof. - Independence boundary: Separate post-turn session/role and verification mandate, capable of outcome readback and challenge rather than repeating the original answer. It inherits context and may use the same model; independence is functional and limited, not a security separation. - Who acts on findings: Reflector for one bounded allowed repair; otherwise originating task/current operator via recap and attention request. - Evidence: experience/reflect.lisp reflection-prompt/fork-reflection; experience/cell.lisp installed observer/worker and repair setting. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for this optional mode. - Caveats: Cached inherited history can bias the reflector. Goal judges, ordinary runtime tests, candidate races and external PR triage are not separately counted as audit witnesses.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence at the declared installed-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible shipped regulatory paths were checked below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material function-specific closure established. - Decision owner: No credited first-party owner for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutable definitions, configuration and operator controls support operation without establishing this function. - Closure path: No qualifying shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent access to eval does not by itself establish the separate organizational decision. - Evidence: src/organism/evolve.lisp; src/organism/knowledge.lisp; src/organism/update.lisp; experience/reflect.lisp; cyber-security/README.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the inspected distribution. - Caveats: Downstream deployments can add organizational behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Policy and identity at the declared installed-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible shipped regulatory paths were checked below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material function-specific closure established. - Decision owner: No credited first-party owner for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutable definitions, configuration and operator controls support operation without establishing this function. - Closure path: No qualifying shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent access to eval does not by itself establish the separate organizational decision. - Evidence: src/organism/evolve.lisp; src/organism/profile.lisp; src/gateway/control.lisp; src/engine/turn.lisp; docs/CELLS.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the inspected distribution. - Caveats: Downstream deployments can add organizational behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b06d8ca3f59d03800d9ccb093505bd110b515243/assessments/no/nodecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b06d8ca3f59d03800d9ccb093505bd110b515243\">b06d8ca &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T17:04:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lanl/ursa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">URSA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lanl/ursa/tree/470b841d83e88457d5677fc824aea32e2d5a5e22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 470b841d83e88457d5677fc824aea32e2d5a5e22\">470b841 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: URSA-configured model agents autonomously investigate, plan, write or execute scientific work, calling tools and revising from live results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended research/engineering questions, uncertain search data and computational errors, incomplete or failing tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven ExecutionAgent chooses tool calls and continuation; named participating agents produce task-relevant results from their individual workspaces and tool feedback. - Decision owner: First-party URSA model/tool agent graph; configured backend model supplies inference rather than a separately authored external research harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph execution/checkpoint wrappers, named agent configuration, workspace, session state, tool registry, sandbox/safety checks, provider invocation and optional RAG/MCP support. - Closure path: prompt/task → model decides code/tool action → first-party tool node executes → workspace/tool observation returned → next model decision / final task result. - Boundary reachability: Installed ursa runtime registers and invokes named first-party agents; dashboard registry builds ExecutionAgent/other shipped agents; Agent Team and Symposium use these installed classes for their members and PI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent&#39;s configured LLM picks concrete operations based on observed tool state, rather than only executing a fixed static plan. Shell tools/search models remain dependencies. - Evidence: src/ursa/agents/base.py; src/ursa/agents/executionagent.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursadashboard/registry.py. - Basis: Explicit first-party installed model/tool loop; structural source, not a live provider receipt. - Confidence: High for reachable code path. - Caveats: Requires model/API and actual scientific tools/data for practical work; no task success/performance measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate conflicting concurrent writable output ownership by multiple independent research S1 agents in the shipped optional Symposium mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneously executing researchers writing common filenames, scripts, reports and intermediate scientific artefacts could overwrite/confound each other&#39;s work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party Symposium constructor assigns each configured participant its own child workspace &lt;symposiumworkspace&gt;/&lt;membername&gt; before concurrent invocation, rather than allowing default shared-writable member workspace. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party workspace-topology constructor under configured member identity; no autonomous model-owned inter-S1 arbitration claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BaseEnvironment.memberworkspace, member factory, named/group state, initial asyncio.gather and revision participant workspaces. - Closure path: Distinct S1 member identity → per-member child work path selected → member&#39;s subsequent tool/code operations run against its own default workspace → independent results returned to Symposium → next participant revision again in member workspace. - Boundary reachability: A supported agentsymposium launch via dashboard environment worker instantiates this first-party default; direct AgentSymposiumEnvironment API and packaged sample configuration also exercise it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The concrete coordination choice is built into first-party topology; an agent is not shown dynamically choosing anti-collision strategy. This is C, not A, and not generic message transport. - Distinct S1 units: At least two separately instantiated configured Symposium member agents/nested teams with independent research outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent conflicting writes or result overwrites if distinct S1 participants share the same default working directory and filenames. - Attenuating coordination relation: Per-member child workspaces localize each participant&#39;s default write target; review temporarily switches to parent workspace only for a declared inspection phase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Path context passed to each configured member&#39;s subsequent run directs its tools/output to its own child path; revised artefacts remain scoped and visible for later review. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The assessed relation changes conflicting writable resource ownership for parallel separate researchers, not merely who receives a prompt or when the next step runs. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursa/environments/agentsymposium.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentsymposium.yaml. - Basis: Structural first-party conflict attenuation in shipped optional operating mode. - Confidence: Medium for bounded default-workspace collision reduction. - Caveats: Not a security sandbox or enforced cross-member write prohibition; arbitrary filesystem tools could cross directories. Shared-workspace Agent Team mode does not inherit this claim.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: The model-driven Agent Team PI regulates current team-level research commitments and correction among currently available member agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent partial research results, missed commitments, mismatched specialist expertise and need to reassign/follow up while satisfying the shared current goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-team roster, overall goal and returned member outputs, PI chooses current member assignment, follow-up work or direct synthesis to address gaps. - Decision owner: Configured first-party model-driven PI agent with delegated member tools, not dashboard background scheduler or static script. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team config, named agent/member registry, first-party dynamic delegateto tools, per-member call/result events, optional nested teams and provider model. - Closure path: overall goal + roster → PI discretionary member commitment choice → first-party delegation tool → member tool/model execution → outcome and evidence returned to PI → PI can choose follow-up/revised assignment and final integration. - Boundary reachability: The shipped dashboard worker starts AgentTeamEnvironment; buildpi actually attaches delegation tools to ExecutionAgent, and ainvoke calls PI with whole-team instructions. These are installed, configured first-party behaviors, not external supervisor CLI assumptions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model PI selects and revises team current commitments conditional on returned results. The observation is bounded to task/team scope, not lasting across independent projects or resource budgets; deterministic dispatch executes its choices. - Whole-system current view: Declared task and full configured team roster, plus member results and evidence of previous dispatches in PI conversation context. - Current-control decision scope: Which present team agent tackles a research subproblem, whether to ask a member for correction/additional work, and when the integrated task can close. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py; src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentteam.yaml. - Basis: Explicit reachable opt-in whole-team model decision right with action/return. - Confidence: Medium: prompt-mediated scheduling and follow-up, not guaranteed intervention in every execution or durable institution-wide commitments. - Caveats: A default single Chat/ExecutionAgent has no proven whole-team S3. Static PlanningExecutor step sequencing alone would be insufficient. No preemption of in-flight member calls or sophisticated resource/budget bargaining is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A separate Symposium participant challenges another agent&#39;s research/output claims by independently examining cross-member work, then returns evidence-backed criticism for revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unverified self-reported research correctness, erroneous code or weak evidence that can survive an author-only status and ordinary completion check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer model judges validity, strength, reproducibility and needed corrections of other participants&#39; submitted writeups/artefacts; author model then decides revised work informed by review. - Decision owner: Separately instantiated configured reviewer participant model(s) in the opt-in Agent Symposium environment, for claims made by different producer agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer membership default reviewer: true, actual complete writeup collection, review-only instruction, parent-workspace inspection, independent participant invocation, first-party mapping of review results to each next revision prompt. - Closure path: ordinary member output/writeup → separate reviewer gets other participants&#39; output + parent child-artifact access → reviewer develops challenges/evidence → critique per review round → first-party revision prompts to original writers → changed subsequent member submissions → organizer synthesis. - Boundary reachability: Dashboard environment worker actually creates AgentSymposium from packaged class/config; ainvoke runs initial participant work, explicitly chooses review-enabled members, invokes them on cross-member materials, collects critiques, and executes further revision rounds. The example config has multiple members, including an independent research path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Review judgment is discretionary to a separate model-driven member; the deterministic round scheduler and presenter only transmit findings. A mere reviewwork call on ExecutionAgent or same-channel DeepReview role swap was not used for the positive claim. - Claim being audited: Another participant&#39;s scientific/code writeup is adequate, evidence-based and reproducible. - Ordinary reporting path: Original writer&#39;s answer, file outputs and internal completion review to Symposium. - Complementary access path: Peer model gets all actual participant writeups (including other workers&#39;) and, during review, parent-workspace view of other participants&#39; child files; can inspect or run referenced code to assess reproducibility. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured agent instances, participant identities and task workspaces, reviewed by a different author/participant. Not a separate model vendor by requirement, not a cryptographic or physical write-protect boundary: reviewers may inspect their own outputs, the same provider/model may be reused and no-edit is instruction-only. - Who acts on findings: The Symposium constructor distributes review text to every original participant via their revision prompt; those model agents revise their own submitted writeups, then the organizer receives updated outputs. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/agentsymposium.py; src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursa/environments/config.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentsymposium.yaml; src/ursa/agents/executionagent.py; src/ursa/agents/deepreviewagent.py. - Basis: Source-attested cross-actor complementary read/review/revise feedback loop in installed optional mode. - Confidence: Medium for first-party prompt-enforced agent independence and corrective return. Low for any stronger claim of immutable artifacts, clean-room evidence, adversarial non-collusion or robust gate. - Caveats: No empirical reviewer accuracy, run-time contamination controls, code immutability or guaranteed finding acceptance. The single-agent fail-open completion reviewer is expressly excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No substantiated future/environment-oriented adaptation that changes URSA&#39;s organizational capability and returns it to subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-level research uncertainty, new web/RAG results, changing plan/task hypotheses; those are operational problem-solving rather than future harness adaptability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party model, constructor or eligible parent mode shown selecting longer-horizon change to URSA&#39;s organizational capabilities in response to external trends. - Decision owner: Research-task models for evolving research answers; package maintainers/operator for non-runtime manual changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning reflection, DeepReview iterative solution refinement, optimisation agents and session/checkpoint persistence. - Closure path: These can refine current research output, but no future/environment sensing → proposed change of agent/workflow capability → adopted operational configuration → later run is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific hypothesis evolution, optimization and internal benchmark tasks are not inherently VSM S4 adaptation of the URSA organization. - Evidence: src/ursa/agents/planningagent.py; src/ursa/agents/deepreviewagent.py; src/ursa/agents/optimizationagent.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after checking opt-in team/symposium and adjacent adaptive task sources. - Confidence: Medium-high for current supported operational scope. - Caveats: If an actually installed cross-run capability change loop is evidenced at same pin, re-assess rather than extrapolate from naming.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No supported first-party ultimate identity/policy determination returned to govern the scientific-task organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Configured workspace scope, selected models, shell safety checks, dashboards/users and team config; these are policy inputs and task gates, not evidenced ultimate identity settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer specifies local settings and research goals; no installed model/constructor or eligible parent authority resolves an ultimate organization-level policy/identity conflict and returns binding policy to activity. - Decision owner: Task owner chooses settings; no function-specific S5 owner evidenced in the shipped assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Security checks, validation, model/tool configuration, named agent/team member metadata and optional dashboard auth. - Closure path: No first-party identity/policy issue → recognized ultimate authority → returned authoritative decision → governed next operation was identified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Named groups, access credentials, human research approvals and selection of provider/agent parameters do not automatically establish S5. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/config.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursa/security.py; src/ursadashboard/security.py; src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py. - Basis: Bounded negative review of shipped policy/configuration/operational paths. - Confidence: Medium-high within declared task-level recursion. - Caveats: Los Alamos organizational policy, repository project maintainership, or external credentials are distinct authorities not imported into this task harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/797d290cfc83c2142a6a924a43070d8f66cbc6c0/assessments/ursa.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 797d290cfc83c2142a6a924a43070d8f66cbc6c0\">797d290 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
